REWRITES = {
 'v136_rhi_07': {'stem': 'A 47-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma has 3 years of bilateral nasal obstruction and complete loss of smell. She has used budesonide saline irrigations twice daily for 6 months with good technique and has never had sinus surgery. Endoscopy shows grade 3 polyps bilaterally, and CT shows pansinus opacification. She took two prednisone courses this year with brief relief. What is the most appropriate next step?', 'choices': ['Prescribe 4 weeks of amoxicillin-clavulanate and reassess the polyp grade', 'Offer endoscopic sinus surgery or an add-on biologic after shared decision-making', 'Schedule prednisone bursts every 3 months to keep the polyps suppressed', 'Obtain serum total IgE and blood eosinophils to confirm the diagnosis first'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'She has endoscopically and radiographically confirmed CRSwNP that remains uncontrolled despite adequate topical steroid delivery, plus asthma that signals type 2 disease. At this point both complete ESS and an FDA-approved add-on biologic (dupilumab, omalizumab, mepolizumab or tezepelumab) are reasonable escalations. The choice is individualized by polyp burden, prior surgery, comorbid asthma or AERD, cumulative steroid exposure, cost, access and patient preference. Surgery also opens the sinuses so that topical steroid can reach the mucosa afterward.', 'why_wrong': ['CRSwNP is driven by type 2 inflammation rather than infection; prolonged antibiotics do not shrink polyps and are not recommended without an infectious exacerbation.', 'Correct.', 'Scheduled systemic steroids accumulate bone, metabolic and ocular toxicity; repeated steroid dependence is a reason to escalate to surgery or a biologic.', 'The diagnosis is already established by endoscopy and CT; biomarkers can inform biologic selection but are not diagnostic and should not delay treatment.'], 'board_pearl': 'Uncontrolled CRSwNP despite topical steroid: escalate to ESS or an add-on biologic chosen by phenotype, history and preference.', 'curveball': 'Before you counsel her, she mentions that her chest tightens after taking ibuprofen. What history should specifically raise concern for AERD?', 'curveball_answer': 'AERD is suggested by the combination of asthma, CRSwNP and respiratory reactions within about 30 minutes to 3 hours of aspirin or other COX-1 inhibitors: congestion, rhinorrhea, wheeze, flushing or laryngeal symptoms. Many patients also react to alcohol, especially red wine. Rapid polyp recurrence after surgery and adult-onset asthma reinforce the suspicion, and the diagnosis can be confirmed by supervised aspirin challenge.'},
 'v141_rhi_03': {'stem': 'A 39-year-old man had complete bilateral endoscopic sinus surgery 14 months ago and now has regrown bilateral polyps with anosmia. His asthma began in his early 30s. He reports that ibuprofen causes nasal congestion and wheezing within an hour, and red wine makes his nose run. CT shows bilateral ethmoid and maxillary opacification without heterogeneous densities or bone expansion. Which diagnosis best explains his course?', 'choices': ['Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis', 'Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease', 'Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis', 'Primary ciliary dyskinesia'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'AERD (Samter triad) combines adult-onset asthma, CRSwNP and respiratory reactions to COX-1 inhibitors, caused by dysregulated arachidonic acid metabolism with cysteinyl leukotriene overproduction and reduced protective prostaglandin E2. The NSAID and alcohol reactions plus rapid polyp regrowth after complete surgery are the deciding clues. Recognition matters because AERD predicts aggressive recurrence, warrants avoidance counseling, and opens options such as postoperative aspirin desensitization or a biologic.', 'why_wrong': ['AFRS typically shows heterogeneous hyperdense sinus contents with expansion on CT and does not cause NSAID-triggered bronchospasm.', 'Correct.', 'EGPA causes asthma and polyps but also systemic vasculitis such as neuropathy or rash, and it does not explain NSAID-triggered reactions.', 'PCD presents from early childhood with neonatal respiratory distress, wet cough and otitis, not adult-onset asthma with NSAID reactions.'], 'board_pearl': 'Asthma + nasal polyps + reaction to aspirin/NSAIDs = AERD; expect rapid polyp recurrence after surgery.', 'curveball': 'He wants to know how aspirin desensitization and biologics fit into his care after revision surgery. How would you counsel him?', 'curveball_answer': 'Aspirin desensitization is usually done a few weeks after complete ESS, followed by daily high-dose aspirin to slow polyp regrowth; it requires controlled asthma, adherence and acceptance of GI bleeding risk. Biologics, especially dupilumab, are highly effective in AERD and avoid daily aspirin. The choice is shared, based on asthma control, contraindications, cost and preference, and both pair with continued topical steroid.'},
 'v206_rhi_crswnp_snr': {'stem': 'A 55-year-old man with asthma had complete bilateral sinus surgery with frontal sinusotomies 18 months ago. Postoperative CT shows all sinuses widely opened without residual cells. He correctly uses budesonide irrigations twice daily, yet endoscopy shows grade 2 polyps in both ethmoid cavities and he has needed three prednisone courses this year. Bone density shows osteopenia. What is the best next step?', 'choices': ['Revision surgery with an endoscopic modified Lothrop procedure', 'Long-term low-dose clarithromycin as primary maintenance therapy', 'Add-on biologic therapy targeting his type 2 inflammatory disease', 'Continue prednisone bursts as needed with calcium and vitamin D'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'When surgery has already achieved wide, complete access and topical steroid delivery is adequate, recurrent polyps reflect ongoing type 2 inflammation rather than an anatomic failure. Repeating an anatomically adequate operation without a defined structural target rarely changes the trajectory. His repeated systemic steroid need and osteopenia make steroid-sparing control a priority, so an add-on biologic is the phenotype-directed escalation. Topical therapy continues, and AERD should be asked about because it affects options.', 'why_wrong': ['A Draf III treats frontal outflow failure, but his cavities, including the frontal sinusotomies, are already widely open with no structural target.', 'Long-term macrolides have their best evidence in noneosinophilic CRSsNP and are not an effective primary therapy for type 2 polyp recurrence.', 'Correct.', 'Repeated systemic steroids with established osteopenia are the toxicity that should drive escalation, and supplements do not make continued bursts safe.'], 'board_pearl': 'Recurrent polyps in a widely opened cavity signal inflammation, not anatomy; escalate biology rather than reoperating reflexively.', 'curveball': 'He now reports that aspirin causes wheezing within an hour. How does this change the discussion of aspirin desensitization versus biologic therapy?', 'curveball_answer': 'This makes AERD likely. Aspirin desensitization followed by daily high-dose aspirin is an option because his cavities are already open, but it requires stable asthma, daily adherence and tolerance of GI bleeding risk. Dupilumab has strong efficacy in AERD and avoids daily aspirin. Either is reasonable; choose with him based on asthma control, GI risk, coverage and preference.'},
 'v136_rhi_08': {'stem': 'A 47-year-old woman had endoscopic transsphenoidal resection of a craniopharyngioma 6 days ago; the large dural defect was closed with fascia and fat only. She now has copious clear drainage from the right nostril when leaning forward, and beta-2 transferrin is positive. CT shows pneumocephalus without hemorrhage. She is scheduled for endoscopic re-exploration. Which reconstruction plan is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Free middle turbinate mucosal graft onlay with fibrin sealant', 'Lumbar drain for 5 days in place of operative re-exploration', 'Multilayer closure covered by a pedicled nasoseptal flap', 'Repacking the sphenoid with fat and nonabsorbable nasal packing'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'A postoperative leak through a large defect that communicated with the cisterns or third ventricle behaves as a high-flow leak, and grafts without a blood supply often fail under that pulsatile CSF pressure. Repair requires exposing the true defect margins, a multilayer inlay and onlay closure, and a vascularized cover. The pedicled nasoseptal flap, based on the posterior septal artery, is the workhorse because it heals reliably over large sellar and anterior skull base defects. Lumbar drainage may be an adjunct but does not replace repair.', 'why_wrong': ['A free mucosal graft is adequate for small low-flow defects, but it lacks the blood supply needed to seal a large high-flow defect.', 'A lumbar drain can be an adjunct, but alone it rarely seals a large postoperative high-flow defect and adds infection and overdrainage risk.', 'Correct.', 'Simple repacking repeats the avascular repair that already failed and does not define the defect or add vascularized tissue.'], 'board_pearl': 'High-flow skull base defects need multilayer reconstruction with vascularized tissue; the nasoseptal flap is the workhorse.', 'curveball': 'The resident asks which artery supplies the nasoseptal flap and where it is most vulnerable during the approach. What do you answer?', 'curveball_answer': 'The flap is pedicled on the posterior septal artery, a terminal branch of the sphenopalatine artery from the internal maxillary artery. The pedicle crosses the face of the sphenoid between the sphenoid ostium and the choana, so wide sphenoidotomy drilled inferiorly, or posterior septectomy, can divide it. Protect or raise the pedicle before opening the sphenoid widely.'},
 'v142_rhi_01': {'stem': 'A 58-year-old man is having an expanded endonasal transplanum resection of a suprasellar meningioma. After tumor removal there is a 2-cm dural defect, the suprasellar cistern is widely open, and CSF wells briskly into the field. A right nasoseptal flap was harvested at the start of the case and is stored in the nasopharynx. Which closure step most directly lowers his postoperative leak risk?', 'choices': ['Fascia lata inlay with an onlay free mucosal graft and sealant', 'Abdominal fat plug placed through the defect with fibrin glue', 'Multilayer graft repair covered by the vascularized septal flap', 'Fascia inlay alone with a lumbar drain for five postoperative days'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Opening an arachnoid cistern or ventricle creates a high-flow leak, the setting in which avascular grafts fail most often. Multilayer reconstruction capped with a pedicled nasoseptal flap supplied by the posterior septal artery lowers postoperative leak rates to low single digits in most series. The flap must lie directly on denuded bone surrounding the defect, without mucosa beneath it, to adhere and heal. Free grafts, fat and fibrin sealant are useful layers but are not substitutes for vascularized tissue.', 'why_wrong': ['This combination suits small low-flow leaks but lacks the blood supply that makes high-flow closure reliable.', 'A fat plug can be one layer, but alone it fills dead space without the vascularized coverage that most reduces high-flow failure.', 'Correct.', 'Lumbar drainage may help as an adjunct after high-flow repair, but it cannot compensate for a single avascular layer over a large defect.'], 'board_pearl': 'Cisternal or ventricular opening means high flow: close in layers and cap with a pedicled nasoseptal flap.', 'curveball': 'In another patient, a high-flow leak is unexpected and no flap was harvested. What step at the start of the case would have preserved this option?', 'curveball_answer': 'A rescue flap. At the start of a sellar approach, the superior and inferior incisions are made and the posterior septal pedicle is elevated off the sphenoid face before the sphenoidotomy is widened, preserving the vascular supply. If a high-flow leak occurs, the incisions are extended anteriorly to complete a full nasoseptal flap; if not, the mucosa is laid back with little morbidity.'},
 'v206_rhi_csf_snr': {'stem': 'A 63-year-old man needs an endoscopic transplanum and transtuberculum approach for a recurrent meningioma, and a high-flow anterior skull base defect is expected. His first operation, 4 years ago, included a posterior septectomy that removed the posterior septum and both septal pedicles. Endoscopy confirms no usable posterior septal mucosa. His frontal scalp and pericranium are intact. Which reconstruction plan is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Multilayer closure with a free septal mucosal graft and sealant', 'Inferior turbinate flap as the only vascularized cover of the planum', 'Multilayer closure covered by an endoscope-assisted pericranial flap', 'Fat and fascia graft followed by a lumbar drain for several days'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'A high-flow defect still needs multilayer closure with vascularized tissue, so reconstruction must be planned before exposure whenever the standard flap may be gone. With both septal pedicles sacrificed, the pericranial flap, based on the supraorbital and supratrochlear vessels and passed through a small nasion osteotomy, provides a large, reliable cover with good reach to the planum and tuberculum. Choosing the flap depends on defect location, prior surgery, tumor involvement and the remaining vascular pedicles.', 'why_wrong': ['Free grafts are inadequate as the definitive cover for a large high-flow defect because they lack a blood supply.', 'The inferior turbinate flap is small and reaches the clivus and sella best; it rarely covers a planum and tuberculum defect.', 'Correct.', 'Avascular grafts with a drain repeat a failure-prone approach and leave the high-flow defect without vascularized coverage.'], 'board_pearl': 'No nasoseptal flap for a high-flow anterior skull base defect: plan a pericranial or other regional vascularized flap.', 'curveball': 'Suppose instead his expected defect were at the lower clivus rather than the planum. Which intranasal vascularized flap would be the better alternative?', 'curveball_answer': 'The inferior turbinate flap, pedicled on the posterior lateral nasal artery from the sphenopalatine artery, suits clival and sellar defects because its pivot point lies low and posterior. It is relatively small, so bilateral flaps may be needed for wider clival defects. The temporoparietal fascia flap passed through the pterygopalatine fossa is another regional option.'},
 'v136_rhi_09': {'stem': 'A 44-year-old woman has persistent right postnasal drainage 1 year after a maxillary antrostomy elsewhere. Endoscopy shows a large surgical opening in the posterior fontanelle and an intact anterior uncinate. Mucus is seen streaming out of the natural ostium hidden behind the uncinate, along the lateral wall, and back into the sinus through the posterior opening. CT shows mucosal thickening of the maxillary sinus only. What best explains her symptoms?', 'choices': ['Odontogenic infection from a carious right maxillary molar', 'Secondary ciliary dyskinesia after mucosal stripping', 'Mucus recirculation between two unconnected openings', 'Osteitis of the maxillary sinus walls from prior surgery'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Mucociliary clearance drives maxillary mucus toward the natural ostium regardless of where a surgical opening is placed. When an antrostomy is created in the posterior fontanelle and does not include the natural ostium, mucus exits the natural ostium and is swept back in through the second opening, producing a loop of persistent drainage and infection. The observed endoscopic flow pattern is the deciding clue. Revision removes the residual uncinate and joins both openings into one antrostomy.', 'why_wrong': ['Odontogenic disease usually causes foul unilateral drainage with dental findings and a tooth-related CT lesion, which are absent here.', 'Stripping can impair clearance, but this cannot explain mucus visibly cycling between the natural ostium and the posterior opening.', 'Correct.', 'Osteitis appears as thickened, sclerotic bone on CT and would not produce the directional mucus loop seen on endoscopy.'], 'board_pearl': 'An antrostomy that misses the natural ostium causes recirculation; revise by removing residual uncinate and connecting the openings.', 'curveball': 'During revision, the resident widens the antrostomy superiorly toward the orbital floor. What orbital structure is at risk with overly aggressive superior dissection?', 'curveball_answer': 'The orbital floor and the orbit above it, including periorbita and orbital fat, sit directly above the antrostomy, and the infraorbital nerve runs in the roof of the sinus. Aggressive superior widening risks orbital penetration, fat prolapse, inferior rectus injury and hematoma. Enlarge posteriorly and inferiorly instead, and keep the orbital floor in view.'},
 'v143_rhi_03': {'stem': 'A 36-year-old man has 6 months of left maxillary pressure and purulent drainage despite irrigations and topical steroid. CT shows left maxillary and infundibular opacification with a patent accessory ostium in the posterior fontanelle; the natural ostium sits anteriorly, obstructed by a thickened uncinate process. He is consented for primary left maxillary antrostomy. Which operative plan best prevents persistent postoperative drainage?', 'choices': ['Remove the uncinate and join the natural and accessory ostia', 'Enlarge the accessory ostium posteriorly and keep the uncinate', 'Create an inferior meatal antrostomy for gravity-dependent drainage', 'Perform balloon dilation of the accessory ostium in the posterior fontanelle'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Cilia carry maxillary mucus toward the natural ostium, located in the ethmoid infundibulum behind the uncinate. A functional antrostomy therefore removes the uncinate, identifies the natural ostium and connects it with any accessory ostium into a single opening. Leaving the natural ostium isolated while enlarging the accessory opening invites recirculation, a well-described cause of failed maxillary surgery. The deciding clue is the obstructed anterior natural ostium separate from a posterior accessory ostium.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Enlarging only the accessory opening leaves the natural ostium isolated behind the uncinate and creates a recirculation circuit.', 'Inferior meatal windows ignore the mucociliary pathway, which still carries mucus to the natural ostium, and they commonly fail.', 'Dilating the accessory ostium does not address the uncinate obstructing the natural ostium and can worsen recirculation.'], 'board_pearl': 'Maxillary cilia ignore surgical windows: remove the uncinate and make one opening that includes the natural ostium.', 'curveball': 'Where is the nasolacrimal duct relative to the anterior antrostomy, and how does that limit anterior enlargement?', 'curveball_answer': 'The nasolacrimal duct runs in its bony canal just anterior to the natural maxillary ostium, roughly 3 to 6 mm in front of it, and opens under the inferior turbinate. Aggressive back-biting anteriorly can transect it and cause epiphora. Enlarge the antrostomy posteriorly and inferiorly, where tissue is safer, and stop anteriorly when hard lacrimal bone is reached.'},
 'v206_rhi_max_app': {'stem': 'During a primary right maxillary antrostomy in a 52-year-old woman, a resident removes the inferior uncinate and sees a round opening into the sinus posterior to where the natural ostium was expected. The resident proposes enlarging it posteriorly as the antrostomy. The natural ostium has not yet been clearly seen, and CT showed a small Haller cell. What is the safest next step?', 'choices': ['Enlarge the visible opening posteriorly and inferiorly as the antrostomy', 'Look for the natural ostium with an angled scope and join the two openings', 'Puncture the canine fossa to confirm the sinus lumen before proceeding', 'Open the Haller cell superiorly first to expose the true natural ostium'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Accessory ostia in the posterior fontanelle are common and easily mistaken for the natural ostium. The natural ostium lies anteriorly in the infundibulum, at about the level of the inferior third of the uncinate, and is often only visible with an angled endoscope after uncinate removal. A functional antrostomy identifies the natural ostium, for example with a curved probe or 30- to 45-degree scope, and connects it to the accessory opening to prevent recirculation.', 'why_wrong': ['Assuming the first visible opening is natural leaves the true ostium isolated and creates the classic mucus recirculation failure.', 'Correct.', 'Canine fossa puncture is reserved for anterior or lateral disease that cannot be reached endoscopically, not for orientation in routine antrostomy.', 'A Haller cell sits along the orbital floor, so aggressive superior dissection to it before finding the ostium risks orbital entry.'], 'board_pearl': 'The first maxillary opening you see is often accessory; find the natural ostium and connect them.', 'curveball': 'How can a Haller cell or a lateralized uncinate distort the expected location of the infundibulum and natural ostium?', 'curveball_answer': 'A Haller (infraorbital ethmoid) cell sits along the medial orbital floor and narrows the infundibulum from above and laterally, displacing the ostium. A lateralized uncinate lies against the lamina papyracea, obliterating the infundibulum and increasing the risk of entering the orbit during uncinectomy. In both cases, study coronal CT, palpate behind the uncinate gently, and use an angled scope to find the natural ostium.'},
 'v206_rhi_max_snr': {'stem': 'During revision left maxillary antrostomy in a 61-year-old man, the natural ostium is scarred and the orbital floor sits unusually low on CT. As the resident widens the superior edge with a microdebrider, yellow globular fat bulges into the field. The globe is covered by drapes. Blood pressure and heart rate are stable. What is the best immediate action?', 'choices': ['Stop the microdebrider, uncover the eye and assess the orbit', 'Debride the bulging fat flush with the antrostomy to restore exposure', 'Place firm ribbon gauze packing against the defect and continue elsewhere', 'Convert to a Caldwell-Luc approach to finish the antrostomy safely'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Fat in the maxillary field means the orbital boundary has been breached. The immediate priority is preventing a small defect from becoming an extraocular muscle, globe or hematoma injury, because microdebriders rapidly draw orbital contents into the blade. Stop powered instrumentation, do not manipulate the fat, uncover the eye to check for proptosis, tension and pupil changes, ballot the globe while watching the defect, and then re-establish landmarks before deciding how far to continue.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Resecting prolapsed orbital fat risks pulling the inferior or medial rectus into the blade and causing permanent diplopia.', 'Firm packing against an orbital defect raises orbital pressure and can worsen a developing hematoma.', 'Conversion to an open approach is not needed for a small breach and adds morbidity before the injury has been assessed.'], 'board_pearl': 'Fat in the field: stop the microdebrider, leave the fat alone, and examine the eye immediately.', 'curveball': 'In recovery, what eye findings would mandate urgent ophthalmologic assessment for orbital hematoma or muscle injury?', 'curveball_answer': 'Increasing proptosis, a tense or firm globe, eyelid ecchymosis that is expanding, pain, decreased vision, a relative afferent pupillary defect or color desaturation suggest hematoma and require immediate decompression and urgent ophthalmology review. New diplopia or restricted gaze, especially on downgaze or abduction, suggests inferior or medial rectus injury and needs early ophthalmology evaluation and CT.'},
 'v136_rhi_10': {'stem': 'A 42-year-old man has new clear, salty drainage from the left nostril 3 days after bilateral endoscopic ethmoidectomy. It increases when he bends forward. Beta-2 transferrin is positive. Preoperative CT showed a Keros type III configuration on the left, with the cribriform plate lying well below the ethmoid roof. Which site was most likely injured?', 'choices': ['Lateral lamella of the cribriform plate', 'Posterior table of the frontal sinus', 'Lamina papyracea near the basal lamella', 'Planum sphenoidale above the optic canal'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'The skull base, comprising the fovea ethmoidalis and the cribriform plate, is the superior boundary of the ethmoid dissection. In a Keros type III configuration the olfactory fossa is deep and the lateral lamella is long and very thin, making it the most common site of iatrogenic CSF leak during ethmoidectomy. Medial dissection near the middle turbinate attachment is especially risky. The positive beta-2 transferrin confirms CSF, and the deep olfactory fossa is the deciding clue.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'The posterior table is involved in frontal sinus surgery, but the deep Keros III olfactory fossa points to the medial ethmoid roof.', 'The lamina papyracea is the lateral orbital boundary; injury there causes fat exposure or hematoma, not CSF rhinorrhea.', 'The planum is skull base, but it lies beyond the sphenoid face and is less commonly injured than the thin lateral lamella during ethmoidectomy.'], 'board_pearl': 'Keros III means a long, thin lateral lamella: the commonest site of iatrogenic CSF leak during ethmoidectomy.', 'curveball': 'Why does a low-lying, asymmetric skull base increase the operative risk of ethmoidectomy?', 'curveball_answer': 'A low skull base shortens the vertical space between the ethmoid cells and the anterior cranial fossa, so a partition that looks like ethmoid bone may actually be skull base. Asymmetry means the contralateral side cannot serve as a template. Review coronal and sagittal CT before surgery, identify the skull base posteriorly first, and dissect laterally away from the lateral lamella.'},
 'v143_rhi_04': {'stem': 'A 34-year-old woman with CRSwNP is undergoing her first complete ethmoidectomy, and the attending asks the junior resident to name the boundaries before passing the basal lamella. Coronal CT shows symmetric Keros type II skull base and an intact lamina papyracea bilaterally. Which pair correctly identifies the superior and lateral limits of the dissection?', 'choices': ['Basal lamella superiorly and lamina papyracea laterally', 'Fovea ethmoidalis superiorly and lamina papyracea laterally', 'Fovea ethmoidalis superiorly and middle turbinate laterally', 'Cribriform plate superiorly and infraorbital nerve laterally'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Safe ethmoidectomy depends on continuous three-dimensional orientation to two danger boundaries. The fovea ethmoidalis, the roof of the ethmoid formed by the frontal bone, is the superior boundary, and breaching it risks CSF leak or brain injury. The lamina papyracea is the lateral boundary, and breaching it risks orbital fat, medial rectus injury or hematoma. The basal lamella of the middle turbinate divides anterior from posterior ethmoid cells rather than limiting the dissection.', 'why_wrong': ['The basal lamella separates anterior and posterior ethmoid cells; it is a vertical landmark crossed during surgery, not the superior limit.', 'Correct.', 'The middle turbinate is the medial boundary of the ethmoid dissection, not the lateral one.', 'The cribriform plate lies medial to the ethmoid roof, and the infraorbital nerve belongs to the maxillary roof, not the ethmoid limits.'], 'board_pearl': 'Ethmoidectomy is bounded by skull base above, lamina papyracea laterally and middle turbinate medially.', 'curveball': 'Moments later, yellow fat suddenly bulges into the ethmoid field. What should the resident stop doing, and what immediate checks are appropriate?', 'curveball_answer': 'Stop powered dissection and do not grasp or debride the fat, which risks medial rectus injury. Uncover the eye, check for proptosis, globe tension and pupil changes, and gently press on the globe while watching the defect to define the breach. If the orbit is soft and vision is unaffected, dissection may continue cautiously away from the defect; check eye movements after waking.'},
 'v206_rhi_eth_app': {'stem': 'A 48-year-old man is scheduled for right ethmoidectomy for CRSsNP. Coronal CT shows a markedly asymmetric skull base: the right fovea ethmoidalis sits about 6 mm lower than the left, with a deep olfactory fossa and long lateral lamella on the right. The left side is Keros type I. Image guidance is available. How should this finding change the right ethmoid dissection?', 'choices': ['Use the left fovea height as the depth reference for clearing the right roof', 'Begin the superior dissection anteriorly and medially at the middle turbinate root', 'Confirm the skull base posteriorly first and follow it forward, staying lateral', 'Rely on image guidance to clear the roof cells so CT review can be skipped'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Skull base height is often asymmetric, so the contralateral side is not a reliable template, and a low skull base narrows the safe vertical corridor. The usual strategy is to identify the skull base where it is easiest to recognize, in the posterior ethmoid or sphenoid roof, and then dissect anteriorly along it. Staying lateral, toward the orbit, avoids the thin lateral lamella medially, which is the commonest site of skull base injury. Image guidance supplements, but does not replace, careful CT review and anatomy.', 'why_wrong': ['The right roof is 6 mm lower, so using the left side as a depth guide would carry instruments straight through the right skull base.', 'The middle turbinate root and lateral lamella are the most fragile and medial parts of the roof; starting there invites CSF leak.', 'Correct.', 'Image guidance has registration error and is an adjunct; it does not replace preoperative study of the CT and anatomic landmarks.'], 'board_pearl': 'Asymmetric low skull base: find the roof posteriorly, follow it forward, and stay lateral to avoid the lateral lamella.', 'curveball': 'Which relationship of the anterior ethmoid artery to the skull base becomes especially important in a low or dehiscent ethmoid roof?', 'curveball_answer': 'With a low roof or long lateral lamella, the anterior ethmoid artery may hang below the skull base in a bony mesentery rather than lying within the roof. It is then exposed to injury during frontal recess and anterior ethmoid dissection. Look for it on coronal CT, where the anterior ethmoid notch on the medial orbital wall marks its exit, and dissect around it gently.'},
 'v206_rhi_eth_snr': {'stem': 'During left anterior ethmoidectomy in a 57-year-old woman, brisk arterial bleeding erupts high at the skull base just behind the frontal recess. The bleeding vessel appears to retract laterally into the lamina papyracea, and the bleeding then slows. Blood pressure is stable. The eyes have been taped and covered since induction. Before continuing the dissection, what is the most important next step?', 'choices': ['Pack the anterior ethmoid tightly with absorbable packing and resume', 'Uncover the eye and assess proptosis, globe tension and pupils', 'Continue dissection carefully and examine the eye in recovery', 'Obtain intraoperative CT angiography before any further dissection'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'A divided anterior ethmoid artery can retract through the lamina into the orbit and bleed there, causing a rapidly expanding retrobulbar hematoma. Raised orbital pressure compresses the optic nerve and its blood supply, and irreversible vision loss can occur within about 60 to 90 minutes. The deciding clue is lateral retraction of the vessel. The eye must be uncovered and examined now; tense proptosis, a firm globe or pupillary change mandates immediate lateral canthotomy and cantholysis.', 'why_wrong': ['Tight packing can drive blood into the orbit and raise orbital pressure, worsening a hematoma rather than controlling it.', 'Correct.', 'Waiting until recovery wastes the window for decompression; an orbital hematoma can blind the eye within the hour.', 'Imaging delays bedside assessment and treatment; orbital hematoma is a clinical diagnosis managed immediately.'], 'board_pearl': 'Anterior ethmoid artery retracting laterally means possible orbital hematoma: uncover and examine the eye immediately.', 'curveball': 'The eyelids are now tense, the globe is proptotic and firm, and the pupil is sluggish. Which actions should follow immediately, and in what order?', 'curveball_answer': 'Perform lateral canthotomy with inferior cantholysis at the bedside, confirming the lower lid becomes lax. Remove any nasal packing, and consider endoscopic medial orbital decompression with incision of periorbita to control the artery. Give adjuncts such as mannitol, acetazolamide and steroids per protocol, and obtain urgent ophthalmology assessment with intraocular pressure and visual acuity measurement.'},
 'v136_rhi_11': {'stem': 'A 32-year-old woman has 4 years of recurrent left cheek and forehead pressure lasting 1 to 2 days, often around menses or after poor sleep, with nasal stuffiness and tearing during attacks. She has taken six antibiotic courses with inconsistent benefit. Nasal endoscopy and sinus CT, obtained between episodes, are normal. Her neurologic examination is normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?', 'choices': ['Recurrent acute bacterial rhinosinusitis', 'Chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps', 'Migraine without aura', 'Trigeminal neuralgia of the second division'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Migraine is the most common diagnosis in patients who label themselves as having sinus headache. Attacks lasting hours to days with hormonal and sleep triggers fit migraine, and trigeminal-autonomic activation can produce congestion and tearing that patients attribute to the sinuses. CRS requires objective inflammation on endoscopy or CT, and recurrent acute rhinosinusitis requires documented episodes with purulence or imaging findings. The absence of any objective sinonasal disease redirects the diagnosis.', 'why_wrong': ['Recurrent ARS requires four or more documented episodes with purulent drainage or imaging findings, which have never been demonstrated here.', 'The diagnosis of CRS requires objective inflammation on endoscopy or CT, and both are normal.', 'Correct.', 'Trigeminal neuralgia causes seconds-long electric shocks with trigger zones, not pressure lasting a day or two with autonomic features.'], 'board_pearl': 'Most self-diagnosed sinus headache is migraine; pain location is anatomy, not etiology.', 'curveball': 'Which accompanying symptoms would support migraine over sinusitis in a patient presenting with facial pressure?', 'curveball_answer': 'Photophobia, phonophobia, nausea, throbbing quality, worsening with routine physical activity, and triggers such as menses, weather, sleep loss or certain foods support migraine, as does a family history and response to triptans. Sinusitis is suggested instead by purulent drainage, smell loss, fever and objective findings on endoscopy or CT. Clear rhinorrhea and congestion can occur in both.'},
 'v144_rh_06': {'stem': 'A 29-year-old man requests sinus surgery for 2 years of severe, episodic throbbing pain behind the right eye and cheek. Attacks last 8 to 24 hours with nausea and light sensitivity, and he lies still in a dark room until they pass. Nasal endoscopy is normal, and a sinus CT obtained during an attack is clear. Three antibiotic courses did not help. What is the best next step?', 'choices': ['Endoscopic sinus surgery to address possible microscopic disease', 'Frontal sinus trephination to measure intrasinus pressure', 'Six weeks of doxycycline for presumed chronic rhinosinusitis', 'Treat as migraine with acute and preventive therapy or neurology referral'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Facial pressure is nonspecific. Episodic throbbing pain lasting hours with nausea and photophobia meets criteria for migraine, and a normal CT obtained during an attack essentially rules out a rhinogenic cause. The deciding clue is the clear imaging at the time of pain. Management redirects to migraine care, such as a triptan or NSAID for attacks and preventive therapy if frequent, or referral to neurology, and it spares the patient unnecessary surgery and antibiotics.', 'why_wrong': ['Sinus surgery without objective disease does not cure migraine and exposes him to complications with no target.', 'Trephination is an operative procedure for frontal sinus disease, not a diagnostic test for pain with a clear CT.', 'Antibiotics are inappropriate without objective inflammation and have already failed three times.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'A clear CT during an attack of facial pain essentially excludes rhinogenic pain; treat the headache disorder.', 'curveball': 'What rare anatomic situations can produce truly rhinogenic contact-point pain, and why must other causes still be excluded?', 'curveball_answer': 'Mucosal contact points, such as a septal spur against the middle or inferior turbinate or a concha bullosa touching the septum, have been proposed as pain sources. Evidence is weak, contact points are common in people without pain, and migraine often coexists. Before surgery, require a concordant clinical picture, exclude primary headache disorders, and consider a topical anesthetic trial at the contact point.'},
 'v207_rhi_pain_app': {'stem': 'A 38-year-old woman has monthly attacks of right-sided facial pressure with ipsilateral nasal congestion, clear rhinorrhea and eyelid swelling, lasting most of a day and worse with exertion. She is certain her sinuses are the cause. Her primary physician obtained a CT 3 years ago that was normal. You examine her during an attack. Which finding most strongly argues against a rhinogenic cause?', 'choices': ['Clear, watery rhinorrhea from the right side during the attack', 'Normal nasal endoscopy performed during the symptomatic episode', 'Pain that worsens when she bends forward or exerts herself', 'Tenderness when the right maxillary sinus wall is palpated'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Migraine can activate the trigeminal-autonomic reflex and cause ipsilateral congestion, clear rhinorrhea and eyelid edema that convince patients their sinuses are responsible. These autonomic signs, positional worsening and facial tenderness are all nonspecific. What separates rhinogenic pain is objective sinonasal inflammation, such as purulence, edema or polyps on endoscopy or mucosal disease on CT, at the time of symptoms. Normal endoscopy during an attack is the strongest evidence against rhinosinusitis and redirects the evaluation toward a primary headache disorder.', 'why_wrong': ['Clear rhinorrhea is a cranial autonomic symptom common in migraine and cluster headache, so it does not distinguish the two.', 'Correct.', 'Worsening with bending or exertion occurs in migraine as well as sinusitis and is not specific for either.', 'Facial tenderness is nonspecific and is commonly present during migraine attacks due to allodynia.'], 'board_pearl': 'Autonomic nasal symptoms occur in migraine; only objective inflammation during symptoms establishes rhinogenic pain.', 'curveball': "Which autonomic nasal symptoms can occur during migraine and falsely reinforce a patient's belief that the pain is sinus-generated?", 'curveball_answer': 'Nasal congestion, clear rhinorrhea, lacrimation, conjunctival injection and eyelid edema can all accompany migraine through activation of the trigeminal-autonomic reflex and parasympathetic outflow. Up to about half of migraine sufferers report at least one. Because these resemble sinus symptoms and the pain often sits over the sinuses, patients and clinicians frequently mislabel migraine as sinus headache.'},
 'v207_rhi_pain_snr': {'stem': 'A 45-year-old woman with long-standing migraine is referred after a CT ordered for headaches showed a 12-mm dome-shaped retention cyst in the floor of the left maxillary sinus. Her headaches are bifrontal and unchanged for 10 years. She has no nasal obstruction, purulent drainage or smell loss, and endoscopy is normal. She asks for surgery to cure her headaches. What is the best counseling approach?', 'choices': ['Explain the cyst is incidental and continue migraine-directed care', 'Offer endoscopic cyst removal because it may be the pain generator', 'Offer bilateral maxillary antrostomies to prevent future attacks', 'Repeat CT in 3 months and operate if the cyst has enlarged'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Maxillary retention cysts are found incidentally in about 10% or more of adults on CT, rarely cause symptoms, and often regress spontaneously. Her headache pattern predates and does not localize to the cyst, and she has no obstructive or inflammatory symptoms. Senior decision-making separates radiographic coincidence from disease that explains symptoms. Surgery should target a defined obstructive, inflammatory, infectious or structural mechanism, not an imaging finding that does not fit the clinical syndrome.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Removing an incidental floor cyst does not relieve bifrontal migraine and adds operative risk without a concordant target.', 'Antrostomies do not prevent migraine, and there is no maxillary outflow disease to treat.', 'Interval imaging is unnecessary for an asymptomatic retention cyst and reinforces the mistaken link to her headaches.'], 'board_pearl': 'Incidental retention cysts do not explain headache; operate only when objective disease matches the symptoms.', 'curveball': 'What rare scenario would make a rhinogenic contribution more plausible, and what cautions remain before surgery?', 'curveball_answer': 'Sinus barotrauma is one example: pain reliably provoked by flying or diving, with a cyst or mucosal disease obstructing an ostium, suggests a pressure-related mechanism. A cyst large enough to block the maxillary ostium is another. Even then, confirm the symptoms are concordant and reproducible, treat coexisting migraine, and counsel that headache relief after surgery is not guaranteed.'},
 'v136_rhi_12': {'stem': 'A 41-year-old woman with chronic left frontal sinusitis is scheduled for frontal sinusotomy. The junior resident plans to follow the usual corridor. Sagittal and coronal CT show no agger nasi pneumatization but a large suprabulla frontal cell that extends along the skull base into the frontal sinus, with a low-hanging anterior ethmoid artery behind it. Where should the frontal drainage pathway be expected?', 'choices': ['Posterior to the cell, running along the skull base', 'Lateral to the cell, against the lamina papyracea', 'Medial to the cell, through an intersinus septal cell', 'Anterior to the cell, behind the frontal beak'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Frontal drainage follows a patient-specific path determined by the surrounding cells, so multiplanar CT must be studied before any dissection. Suprabulla and suprabulla frontal cells lie above the ethmoid bulla, against the skull base, and push the drainage pathway anteriorly. Expecting the pathway posteriorly would send instruments toward the skull base and the anterior ethmoid artery. Using the International Frontal Sinus Anatomy Classification to map each cell lets the surgeon open the true pathway safely.', 'why_wrong': ['Posteriorly lies the skull base and anterior ethmoid artery; this cell occupies that space and displaces drainage forward.', 'Supraorbital or lateral cells can push drainage medially, but a suprabulla frontal cell does not displace it toward the lamina.', 'Intersinus septal cells push drainage laterally, and none is described on this CT.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Agger nasi and supra agger cells push drainage posteriorly and medially; suprabulla cells push it anteriorly.', 'curveball': "Postoperatively, the resident strips the frontal recess mucosa circumferentially to 'prevent polyps.' What is the consequence of this in the frontal outflow tract?", 'curveball_answer': 'Circumferential stripping exposes bone in a narrow outflow tract, triggering osteitis, granulation, neo-osteogenesis and concentric scar that restenose the frontal drainage pathway. It is a leading cause of iatrogenic frontal sinus obstruction and mucocele. Preserve mucosa, use through-cutting instruments, remove bony partitions rather than mucosa, and maintain postoperative irrigation and debridement.'},
 'v144_rh_07': {'stem': 'A 53-year-old man with chronic frontal sinusitis and polyps is scheduled for bilateral frontal sinusotomy. On preoperative review of coronal and sagittal CT, the attending points out a large supraorbital ethmoid cell on the right, a steep anterior skull base slope, and a prominent notch on the medial orbital wall below the skull base. Which structure is at greatest risk during right frontal recess dissection?', 'choices': ['Anterior ethmoid artery lying in a mesentery below the roof', 'Nasolacrimal duct within the frontal process of the maxilla', 'Posterior ethmoid artery at the sphenoethmoidal junction', 'Optic nerve running through a pneumatized anterior clinoid process'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'A supraorbital ethmoid cell, a steep skull base and a low anterior ethmoid notch on CT predict that the anterior ethmoid artery runs free below the skull base in a bony mesentery. During frontal recess dissection it can be avulsed, retracting into the orbit and causing a hematoma, or the adjacent lateral lamella can be breached. Preoperative mapping of cells and the artery on multiplanar CT is therefore essential before any superior dissection in the frontal recess.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'The nasolacrimal duct is at risk with anterior maxillary antrostomy, not frontal recess dissection behind the agger nasi.', 'The posterior ethmoid artery lies far posterior to the frontal recess and is not threatened by frontal dissection.', 'The optic nerve and clinoid are sphenoid-region risks and are not encountered in frontal recess surgery.'], 'board_pearl': 'Supraorbital ethmoid cells and a low AE notch on CT warn of a free-hanging anterior ethmoid artery.', 'curveball': 'Two years later he develops a laterally based frontal mucocele above the orbit. What does that suggest about access and drainage strategy?', 'curveball_answer': 'A far-lateral frontal mucocele is often beyond the reach of a standard Draf IIa. Options include a Draf IIb or Draf III to widen access with angled instruments, or a combined endoscopic and external approach, such as an above-the-brow trephine or osteoplastic flap, when the lesion lies beyond the endoscopic reach. Marsupialize the mucocele into the nasal cavity rather than obliterating when feasible.'},
 'v207_rhi_frontal_app': {'stem': 'A 37-year-old woman has chronic left frontal sinusitis despite medical therapy. Coronal and sagittal CT show her frontal drainage pathway narrowed by a pneumatized agger nasi cell and a large supra agger frontal cell that reaches the skull base. The frontal ostium itself is of normal width, and there is no lateral frontal disease. Which operative plan is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Drill the frontal beak to a Draf IIb as the primary procedure', 'Strip the recess mucosa completely to reduce recurrent polyps', 'Use a Lynch external frontoethmoidectomy to reach the cells', 'Remove the obstructing cell walls, preserving mucosa, to the ostium'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Modern frontal sinus surgery is based on the patient-specific cell anatomy, not a fixed tube. With a normal ostium and disease caused by agger nasi and supra agger cells, the target is a Draf IIa: remove the cell walls that obstruct the drainage pathway, which lies posterior and medial to these cells, and preserve mucosa to the ostium. Multiplanar CT mapping guides which partitions to remove, and targeted surgery minimizes scarring around the orbit and skull base.', 'why_wrong': ['Drilling the beak is not needed when the ostium is normal and the obstruction comes from removable cells.', 'Mucosal stripping exposes bone and causes osteitis and stenosis, which is the most common cause of frontal failure.', 'External frontoethmoidectomy is largely obsolete for this indication and causes collapse of the orbital wall with stenosis.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Frontal surgery is cell-based: remove obstructing partitions, preserve mucosa, and enlarge only as needed.', 'curveball': 'How does an anterior ethmoid artery hanging below the skull base change safe dissection around the posterior frontal cells?', 'curveball_answer': 'If the artery hangs below the roof, removing posterior cell walls with forceful or powered instruments can avulse it, leading to orbital hematoma or bleeding from the skull base. Identify it on CT and endoscopically, dissect anterior to it, and use gentle through-cutting instruments. If it bleeds, use bipolar cautery while protecting the lamina, and check the eye immediately.'},
 'v207_rhi_frontal_snr': {'stem': 'During revision left frontal sinusotomy in a 50-year-old man, the obstructing frontal cells have been removed and a 5-mm, mucosa-lined frontal outflow tract is now visible with clear mucus flowing. The fellow wants to enlarge it further with a drill, which would denude the circumference near the skull base and orbit. The sinus has no further disease. What is the best decision?', 'choices': ['Drill the outflow to at least 10 mm so it will remain patent', 'Accept the patent, mucosa-lined outflow and end the dissection', 'Convert to a Draf III to create the widest possible opening', 'Place a silicone stent and drill a further 2 mm circumferentially'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The goal of frontal surgery is durable drainage, not maximal diameter at any cost. Circumferential drilling exposes bone, promoting osteitis, neo-osteogenesis and concentric scar that can restenose even a wide opening. When the obstructing disease has been addressed and a mucosa-lined pathway is patent, further enlargement adds restenosis and skull base or orbital risk for little gain. Postoperative irrigation, topical steroid and debridement then maintain patency.', 'why_wrong': ['Circumferential drilling denudes the outflow and increases restenosis risk; an arbitrary diameter target does not guarantee patency.', 'Correct.', 'A Draf III is reserved for failed lesser procedures or specific pathology, not a patent outflow with no residual disease.', 'Stents do not compensate for circumferential bone exposure, and drilling more injures the healthy mucosa that keeps the tract open.'], 'board_pearl': 'A patent mucosa-lined frontal outflow beats a wider denuded one; stop when the disease target is corrected.', 'curveball': 'Which postoperative debridement and topical therapy principles help maintain frontal patency after technically adequate surgery?', 'curveball_answer': 'Use high-volume saline irrigation and topical steroid, often budesonide in the rinse, to control inflammation. Debride crust, clot and early synechiae endoscopically in the first weeks while avoiding trauma to healthy mucosa. Treat early polyps or edema promptly. If scar progressively narrows the tract, early targeted revision is preferable to waiting for complete obstruction.'},
 'v136_rhi_13': {'stem': 'A 59-year-old woman has recalcitrant bilateral frontal sinusitis after two prior frontal sinusotomies. Endoscopy shows dense scar in both frontal recesses, and CT shows a frontal anteroposterior diameter of 14 mm. The attending explains to the resident that she needs a Draf III rather than a Draf IIb. Which feature distinguishes the Draf III from the Draf IIb?', 'choices': ['Removal of the superior septum and intersinus septum to join both sinuses', 'Removal of the frontal sinus floor from the lamina papyracea to the septum', 'Clearance of frontal recess cells up to, but not through, the frontal ostium', 'Obliteration of the frontal sinus with fat through an osteoplastic flap'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Draf procedures describe escalating frontal sinusotomy extent. Draf I clears the ethmoid cells below the frontal ostium, Draf IIa removes the cells and opens the ostium between the lamina papyracea and middle turbinate, and Draf IIb removes the frontal floor from the lamina to the nasal septum on one side. The Draf III, or endoscopic modified Lothrop, adds a superior septectomy and removal of the intersinus septum and both floors, creating a single common opening.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Floor removal from lamina to septum on one side is the definition of a Draf IIb, not the Draf III.', 'Clearing cells without opening the ostium describes a Draf I, the most limited frontal procedure.', 'Osteoplastic obliteration is an external procedure that eliminates the sinus rather than enlarging its drainage.'], 'board_pearl': 'Draf IIa: lamina to middle turbinate; IIb: lamina to septum; III: both sides joined through superior septectomy.', 'curveball': 'Six months after the Draf III, what postoperative problem makes meticulous topical care and debridement important?', 'curveball_answer': 'The large raw drilled bone circumference can heal with neo-osteogenesis and scar that narrows the neo-ostium; most stenosis occurs in the first year. Restenosis rates are highest with exposed bone, eosinophilic disease and poor follow-up. Regular endoscopic debridement, high-volume steroid irrigation and early treatment of inflammation help preserve the opening, and some surgeons use mucosal grafts or flaps over exposed bone.'},
 'v142_rhi_02': {'stem': 'A 64-year-old man has persistent bilateral frontal sinusitis after complete ethmoidectomy and bilateral Draf IIa procedures. Endoscopy shows both frontal recesses closed by scar, and CT shows bony neo-osteogenesis at both frontal ostia with an anteroposterior frontal depth of 15 mm. There is no lateral frontal disease or tumor. Which procedure is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Revision bilateral Draf IIa with mucosal flaps', 'Balloon dilation of both frontal ostia', 'Endoscopic modified Lothrop (Draf III)', 'Osteoplastic flap with frontal obliteration'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'After failed bilateral frontal sinusotomies with neo-osteogenesis, the Draf III removes the frontal floors, intersinus septum and superior septum to create a wide common median opening. Its success depends on an adequate anteroposterior frontal depth, which he has. It preserves the sinus mucosa and allows endoscopic surveillance, unlike obliteration, and is the usual next step for recalcitrant bilateral frontal disease before an external approach is considered.', 'why_wrong': ['Repeating the same limited procedure on both sides is likely to restenose given established neo-osteogenesis.', 'Balloon dilation cannot open ostia narrowed by new bone after prior surgery and has a high failure rate here.', 'Correct.', 'Obliteration is reserved for failure of extended endoscopic procedures or unfavorable anatomy and hides recurrence from endoscopic view.'], 'board_pearl': 'Failed bilateral frontal sinusotomies with neo-osteogenesis and adequate AP depth: consider a Draf III before obliteration.', 'curveball': 'Which structures set the lateral and posterior safety limits during a Draf III?', 'curveball_answer': 'Posteriorly, the first olfactory fibers at the anterior cribriform plate, identified at the anterior attachment of the middle turbinate, mark the limit near the skull base. Laterally, the lamina papyracea and orbit, and the skin of the medial canthus anteriorly, set the limits. Drilling anteriorly on the frontal beak is safe up to the nasal bone and skin.'},
 'v207_rhi_draf_snr': {'stem': 'A 57-year-old woman with eosinophilic CRSwNP had a Draf III 3 years ago. She now has recurrent right frontal pressure. Endoscopy shows that most of the common frontal opening is wide and mucosa-lined, but the right lateral edge is narrowed by scar and new bone, trapping mucus in the right frontal sinus. CT confirms isolated right lateral restenosis. Which revision approach is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Redrilling the entire neo-ostium circumferentially', 'Osteoplastic flap with frontal fat obliteration', 'Balloon dilation of the stenotic right segment', 'Targeted removal of the focal scar and new bone'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Revision frontal surgery should be mechanism-specific. A focal segment of scar and neo-osteogenesis can be removed selectively while preserving the already healthy mucosa and patent neo-ostium. Redrilling the whole opening adds raw bone and can create more restenosis. The underlying eosinophilic inflammation should also be treated, with steroid irrigation and consideration of a biologic, because uncontrolled type 2 disease is a major cause of failure.', 'why_wrong': ['Redrilling healthy, patent segments creates more exposed bone and actually increases the risk of global restenosis.', 'Obliteration is a last resort after failure of extended endoscopic surgery and is excessive for a focal, correctable stenosis.', 'Balloon dilation cannot reliably open a segment narrowed by new bone and is not durable for neo-osteogenic restenosis.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Revise a focal Draf III restenosis selectively and treat the inflammation that drove it.', 'curveball': 'How do neo-osteogenesis, exposed drilled bone, inflammatory phenotype and postoperative care each contribute to restenosis risk?', 'curveball_answer': 'Exposed drilled bone heals by osteoneogenesis and scar contracture, so more bare bone means more narrowing; mucosal grafts or flaps reduce this. Eosinophilic or AERD phenotypes drive persistent edema and polyps. Poor postoperative debridement and inconsistent irrigation allow crust and synechiae to organize. Smaller initial opening size and smaller AP depth also predict stenosis.'},
 'v136_rhi_14': {'stem': 'A 66-year-old woman with hypertension has 8 months of right cheek pressure and intermittent foul postnasal drainage. She is not immunosuppressed and has no diabetes. Endoscopy shows mild edema at the right middle meatus without necrosis. Noncontrast CT shows complete right maxillary opacification with central punctate hyperdensities and thickened sclerotic bony walls. What is the most appropriate treatment?', 'choices': ['Endoscopic middle meatal antrostomy with removal of all debris', 'Twelve weeks of oral voriconazole followed by repeat CT', 'Oral prednisone taper with itraconazole for allergic fungal disease', 'Caldwell-Luc approach with complete stripping of sinus mucosa'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'An immunocompetent older adult with unilateral maxillary opacification, central hyperdensities and wall sclerosis most likely has a fungal ball, a noninvasive mass of hyphae in the sinus lumen. Endoscopic middle meatal antrostomy with complete removal of the concretions and restoration of ventilation is usually curative. Systemic antifungals are unnecessary without tissue invasion, though debris and abnormal mucosa should be sent for histology to exclude invasion or neoplasm.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Antifungal drugs do not penetrate a fungal ball and are not indicated without histologic tissue invasion.', 'AFRS is typically bilateral or multisinus in younger atopic patients with eosinophilic mucin, and steroids do not clear a fungal ball.', 'Mucosal stripping is unnecessary and harmful; endoscopic removal with a wide antrostomy is curative in most cases.'], 'board_pearl': 'Unilateral maxillary opacification with central calcifications in an immunocompetent adult: fungal ball, treat endoscopically.', 'curveball': 'In another patient the identical disease is in the sphenoid sinus. What symptom would make a sphenoid fungal ball particularly concerning?', 'curveball_answer': 'New visual loss, visual field defect or diplopia from optic nerve or cranial nerve III, IV or VI involvement is the red flag, since the sphenoid abuts the optic canal and cavernous sinus. It can reflect compression, inflammation or conversion to invasive disease. It warrants urgent imaging including MRI, ophthalmology input and prompt sphenoidotomy.'},
 'v144_rh_08': {'stem': 'A 58-year-old man has 6 months of left-sided foul nasal drainage and cacosmia. He takes no immunosuppressive drugs, and his glucose is normal. Endoscopy shows thick brown debris at the left middle meatus with healthy-appearing mucosa and no necrosis. CT shows left maxillary opacification with metallic-density concretions. He is scheduled for endoscopic surgery and asks about medicines afterward. What should you tell him?', 'choices': ['Oral itraconazole for 3 months is needed to prevent recurrence', 'IV amphotericin B is needed for 2 weeks after surgery', 'Oral prednisone for a month is needed to control fungal allergy', 'Surgery alone is usually curative, and antifungals are not needed'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'A fungal ball is noninvasive fungal colonization of a sinus lumen. Its metallic-density concretions come from calcium and heavy metals in the fungal mass, which is usually Aspergillus. Complete endoscopic removal and a wide opening for ventilation cure most patients, so systemic antifungal treatment is not routinely given. Saline irrigations help clear residual debris, and histology is reviewed to confirm there is no tissue invasion.', 'why_wrong': ['Itraconazole is used in selected AFRS cases, not for a fungal ball, where it adds hepatotoxicity without benefit.', 'Amphotericin is for invasive fungal sinusitis; it is toxic and not indicated without tissue invasion.', 'Systemic steroids treat the eosinophilic inflammation of AFRS, which a fungal ball does not have.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Fungal ball: noninvasive, cured by complete endoscopic removal; no systemic antifungals unless histology shows invasion.', 'curveball': 'His final histology unexpectedly shows hyphae invading the sinus mucosa. How does management change immediately?', 'curveball_answer': 'This is invasive fungal rhinosinusitis, not a fungal ball. Start systemic antifungal therapy, guided by species, such as voriconazole for Aspergillus, and evaluate immune status, including HIV, glucose and medications. Obtain contrast MRI to define orbital and intracranial extent, return to surgery for debridement as needed, and involve infectious disease.'},
 'v207_rhi_fungalball_app': {'stem': 'A 71-year-old woman with no diabetes or immunosuppression has 4 months of progressive retro-orbital and vertex headache unresponsive to analgesics. Vision and cranial nerves are normal. Endoscopy shows no purulence or mass. CT shows isolated right sphenoid opacification with central calcific hyperdensities and wall sclerosis; MRI shows hypointense sphenoid contents on T2 without extrasinus extension. What is the best management?', 'choices': ['Oral voriconazole for 3 months, then repeat CT scan', 'Endoscopic sphenoidotomy with complete removal', 'Observation with repeat CT in 6 months', 'Oral prednisone and itraconazole as for AFRS'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Hyperdense calcific contents on CT and T2-hypointense material on MRI in an isolated sphenoid of an immunocompetent older woman indicate a fungal ball. Because she is symptomatic and the sphenoid lies next to the optic nerve, carotid artery and cavernous sinus, endoscopic sphenoidotomy with complete removal and a wide opening is indicated. Systemic antifungals are unnecessary without tissue invasion, but debris and mucosa should be sent for histology and the walls inspected for dehiscence.', 'why_wrong': ['Antifungals do not clear a noninvasive fungal ball, and delaying surgery leaves a symptomatic lesion next to the optic nerve.', 'Correct.', 'Symptomatic sphenoid disease adjacent to the optic nerve and cavernous sinus should be cleared rather than observed.', 'Steroids and antifungals treat AFRS, whose eosinophilic mucin pattern and atopy are absent in this isolated calcified sphenoid.'], 'board_pearl': 'Isolated sphenoid hyperdensity with T2 hypointensity in an immunocompetent patient: fungal ball, open and clear the sphenoid.', 'curveball': 'What visual symptom or cranial neuropathy would make sphenoid fungal disease an urgent rather than elective problem?', 'curveball_answer': 'Decreased visual acuity, visual field loss, color desaturation or an afferent pupillary defect suggest optic nerve compromise; diplopia or ptosis suggests cavernous sinus involvement of cranial nerves III, IV or VI; facial numbness suggests V1 or V2. Any of these makes the case urgent, requiring same-day imaging, ophthalmology input and prompt sphenoidotomy with evaluation for invasion.'},
 'v207_rhi_fungalball_snr': {'stem': 'A 68-year-old man with type 2 diabetes (HbA1c 9.4%) is undergoing endoscopic removal of a presumed right sphenoid fungal ball. After the debris is cleared, the sphenoid mucosa appears pale and does not bleed when cut. Frozen section shows fungal hyphae within the submucosa and vessel walls. Vision was normal before surgery. What is the best next decision?', 'choices': ['Complete the debris removal and await final pathology before changing therapy', 'Treat as a fungal ball with saline irrigations and endoscopic follow-up', 'Give oral prednisone and itraconazole for presumed allergic fungal disease', 'Debride to bleeding tissue, start systemic antifungals, and correct glucose'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Tissue invasion is the defining difference between a fungal ball and invasive fungal rhinosinusitis, and the frozen section changes management immediately. Angioinvasion with pale, nonbleeding mucosa in a poorly controlled diabetic suggests acute invasive disease. Management requires debridement to bleeding margins, prompt systemic antifungal therapy (liposomal amphotericin B until species are known), correction of hyperglycemia, urgent MRI for orbital and intracranial extent, and multidisciplinary infectious disease involvement.', 'why_wrong': ['Waiting for final pathology delays debridement and antifungal therapy in a disease that progresses over hours.', 'Irrigations alone are appropriate for a fungal ball, but histologic tissue invasion makes this invasive disease.', 'Steroids worsen invasive fungal infection and hyperglycemia, and this is not allergic fungal disease.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Fungus in tissue on frozen section turns a fungal ball into invasive disease: debride, treat systemically, reverse host factors.', 'curveball': 'Which host factors and orbital or cranial nerve findings most strongly increase concern for rapidly progressive angioinvasive disease?', 'curveball_answer': 'Diabetic ketoacidosis, neutropenia, hematologic malignancy, stem cell or solid-organ transplant, high-dose steroids and iron overload raise the risk. Facial or palatal numbness, black or insensate mucosa, decreasing vision, ophthalmoplegia, proptosis or an afferent pupillary defect signal orbital apex or cavernous sinus spread and demand emergent debridement and imaging.'},
 'v136_rhi_15': {'stem': 'A 42-year-old woman has persistent purulent CRS without polyps 1 year after technically complete ESS with widely patent sinuses. In the past 2 years she has had three radiographically confirmed pneumonias and two episodes of bacterial bronchitis requiring antibiotics. She has no asthma, and allergy testing was negative. Cultures grow pneumococcus and Haemophilus influenzae. What is the best next step?', 'choices': ['Revision ESS with a modified Lothrop to improve access', 'Prophylactic azithromycin three times weekly for 12 months', 'Evaluate humoral immunity before planning further treatment', 'Begin subcutaneous immunotherapy for presumed allergy'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Refractory CRS with patent sinuses after adequate surgery, combined with recurrent pneumonias caused by encapsulated bacteria, should prompt evaluation for a humoral immunodeficiency. Initial testing is quantitative IgG, IgA and IgM with specific antibody titers to vaccines, with immunology referral when abnormal. Identifying a defect such as common variable immunodeficiency or specific antibody deficiency changes treatment, because immunoglobulin replacement or targeted prophylaxis may matter more than further sinus surgery.', 'why_wrong': ['Her sinuses are already widely open, so more surgery would not address the likely systemic host defect.', 'Prophylactic antibiotics may eventually be part of care, but starting them without identifying an immune defect skips the diagnosis.', 'Correct.', 'Her allergy testing was negative, and immunotherapy does not treat recurrent bacterial sinopulmonary infection.'], 'board_pearl': 'Refractory CRS plus recurrent pneumonias with encapsulated organisms: check immunoglobulins and vaccine responses.', 'curveball': 'Before referral, which basic laboratory studies can you order to screen her humoral immunity?', 'curveball_answer': 'Start with quantitative serum IgG, IgA and IgM, IgG subclasses in selected cases, and specific antibody titers to tetanus and diphtheria (protein antigens) and to pneumococcal serotypes (polysaccharide antigens). If pneumococcal titers are low, measure response 4 to 8 weeks after the polysaccharide vaccine. A CBC with differential and HIV test are also reasonable.'},
 'v144_rh_09': {'stem': 'A 33-year-old man has refractory CRS, two prior ESS procedures and recurrent pneumonias. Immunology testing shows IgG 280 mg/dL, low IgA and IgM, and absent responses to tetanus and pneumococcal vaccines. Lymphocyte counts are normal. Endoscopy shows patent sinuses with mucopurulence. He asks whether a third operation will finally fix his sinuses. What is the most important next step?', 'choices': ['Start immunoglobulin replacement coordinated with immunology', 'Schedule a third ESS with wider maxillary megantrostomies', 'Begin daily prophylactic trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole as sole therapy', 'Revaccinate against pneumococcus and repeat titers in 1 year'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Low IgG with low IgA or IgM and absent vaccine responses in an adult with recurrent sinopulmonary infections establishes common variable immunodeficiency once secondary causes are excluded. The host defect is driving his recurrences, so immunoglobulin replacement directed by immunology is the key treatment and reduces pneumonias and sinus infections. Surgery cannot correct the immune deficiency, though topical therapy and culture-directed antibiotics continue alongside it.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'His sinuses are already patent, and another operation would not correct the underlying antibody deficiency.', 'Prophylactic antibiotics may supplement treatment but do not replace immunoglobulin in CVID with very low IgG.', 'He has already shown absent vaccine responses, and waiting a year leaves him unprotected against invasive infection.'], 'board_pearl': 'Low IgG plus low IgA or IgM and poor vaccine response: CVID; immunoglobulin replacement is the core treatment.', 'curveball': 'In a different patient, total IgG, IgA and IgM are normal but pneumococcal responses are poor. How does that change management?', 'curveball_answer': 'That pattern is specific antibody deficiency. Despite normal immunoglobulin levels, the patient cannot make protective antibody to polysaccharide antigens. Management starts with conjugate vaccination, aggressive treatment of infections and sometimes prophylactic antibiotics; immunoglobulin replacement is considered for severe or refractory infections. It explains recurrence that surgery alone will not fix.'},
 'v207_rhi_immune_app': {'stem': 'A 45-year-old woman has refractory CRS without polyps despite ESS and maximal topical therapy. Over 3 years she has had four bacterial pneumonias and an episode of pneumococcal bacteremia. She is HIV negative and takes no immunosuppressive drugs. Her primary physician noted she never mounted a response to a prior hepatitis B vaccine series. Which initial immune evaluation is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Antinuclear antibody and ANCA serologies', 'Sweat chloride testing and nasal nitric oxide', 'Lymphocyte subset flow cytometry alone', 'Immunoglobulins with vaccine antibody titers'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Recurrent sinopulmonary infections with encapsulated bacteria, invasive pneumococcal disease and poor vaccine response point to a humoral immune defect. The appropriate first evaluation is quantitative IgG, IgA and IgM with functional antibody responses to protein and polysaccharide vaccine antigens. Results separate CVID, specific antibody deficiency and other disorders and guide referral. Host defense should be evaluated before attributing every recurrence to surgical failure.', 'why_wrong': ['Autoantibody tests screen for vasculitis or connective tissue disease, not the antibody deficiency suggested by encapsulated infections.', 'Sweat chloride and nasal nitric oxide screen for CF and PCD, which present earlier and with other features.', 'Flow cytometry is a later test for cellular defects; humoral screening with immunoglobulins and titers comes first.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Recurrent encapsulated-organism infections with CRS: screen immunoglobulins and vaccine titers first.', 'curveball': 'How do specific antibody deficiency and common variable immunodeficiency differ in immunoglobulin levels and vaccine responses?', 'curveball_answer': 'In CVID, IgG is markedly low along with low IgA or IgM, and responses to both protein and polysaccharide vaccines are poor. In specific antibody deficiency, IgG, IgA and IgM levels are normal, but responses to pneumococcal polysaccharide antigens are impaired while protein responses are preserved. CVID usually needs immunoglobulin replacement; SAD often starts with vaccination and prophylaxis.'},
 'v207_rhi_immune_snr': {'stem': 'A 38-year-old woman with CVID on stable subcutaneous immunoglobulin replacement still has nasal obstruction and purulent drainage despite optimized irrigations and topical steroid. She has never had sinus surgery. CT shows bilateral obstructed maxillary and ethmoid sinuses with retained secretions, and cultures repeatedly grow resistant Pseudomonas. Her immunologist asks whether surgery has a role. What is the best plan?', 'choices': ['Avoid surgery because CVID prevents adequate mucosal healing', 'Offer ESS for source control, coordinated with immunology and ID', 'Increase immunoglobulin dosing and continue oral antibiotics only', 'Place a PICC line for 6 weeks of IV antipseudomonal antibiotics'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Immune deficiency changes perioperative risk, microbiology and expectations but does not remove the role of surgery when there is obstructed disease with a correctable target. ESS drains retained purulence, provides culture material and opens the sinuses for topical irrigation, which is especially valuable when systemic antibiotics are limited by resistance. Best care pairs host optimization through immunology with culture-directed antimicrobials and surgical source control, rather than choosing one over the other.', 'why_wrong': ['Patients with CVID on replacement heal adequately and benefit from ESS when there is obstructed disease to drain.', 'Correct.', 'Her immunoglobulin replacement is already optimized, and antibiotics cannot clear purulence trapped in obstructed sinuses.', 'Prolonged IV antibiotics without drainage carry line risk and are unlikely to clear obstructed, resistant sinus infection.'], 'board_pearl': 'Immunodeficiency does not preclude ESS; obstructed sinuses still need drainage and topical access alongside host therapy.', 'curveball': 'What perioperative microbiology or prophylaxis issues would you discuss with immunology and ID for a patient colonized with resistant organisms?', 'curveball_answer': 'Obtain intraoperative cultures to guide postoperative antibiotics, consider culture-directed perioperative coverage for her Pseudomonas, and plan topical antibiotic irrigation such as tobramycin or mupirocin in selected cases. Confirm IgG trough levels are adequate before surgery and discuss timing of infusions. Arrange close endoscopic follow-up with repeat cultures.'},
 'v136_rhi_16': {'stem': 'A 34-year-old man has 3 years of bilateral nasal obstruction from allergic rhinitis. Allergen avoidance, a year of fluticasone and an antihistamine controlled his sneezing and itch but not the blockage. Anterior rhinoscopy shows enlarged inferior turbinates that shrink only partly after oxymetazoline, and the septum is straight. The nasal valves are normal. Which surgical approach is preferred?', 'choices': ['Total inferior turbinate resection along its entire length', 'Middle turbinate resection to widen the common meatus', 'Laser ablation of the inferior turbinate mucosal surface', 'Submucosal turbinate reduction with lateral outfracture'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'When enlarged inferior turbinates obstruct despite medical therapy, surgery should reduce the obstructing submucosal soft tissue or bone while preserving the overlying mucosa. Intact mucosa maintains humidification, warming, mucociliary clearance and the airflow sensation that patients perceive as a clear nose. Submucosal techniques such as microdebrider, radiofrequency or bone resection, often with outfracture, achieve durable improvement with low risk of crusting or empty nose symptoms.', 'why_wrong': ['Total resection removes functioning mucosa and risks crusting, bleeding and empty nose syndrome.', 'The middle turbinate is not causing his obstruction, and resecting it risks olfactory loss and frontal recess scarring.', 'Surface laser ablation destroys the mucosa that provides humidification and clearance, causing crusting and scarring.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Turbinate surgery reduces submucosal bulk while preserving mucosa; resect the volume, not the function.', 'curveball': 'Three months after surgery he still feels obstructed, mostly on inspiration. How does dynamic nasal valve collapse change the interpretation?', 'curveball_answer': 'If the lateral nasal wall or alae collapse on inspiration, the obstruction is at the valve, not the turbinates. Confirm by watching quiet and forced inspiration and by a modified Cottle maneuver or lateral wall support with a cerumen curette. Treatment is functional rhinoplasty with lateral crural strut or alar batten grafts, not further turbinate removal.'},
 'v144_rh_10': {'stem': 'A 40-year-old woman with perennial allergic rhinitis has chronic bilateral obstruction despite optimal medical therapy and is scheduled for inferior turbinate reduction. She read online that complete removal gives the best breathing and asks for both turbinates to be removed entirely. Endoscopy shows hypertrophic inferior turbinates and a straight septum. Which long-term complication is the main reason to preserve turbinate mucosa?', 'choices': ['Epiphora from injury to the nasolacrimal duct opening', 'Septal perforation from contact mucosal injury', 'Paradoxical obstruction and crusting of empty nose', 'Anosmia from scarring of the olfactory cleft'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'The inferior turbinates warm, humidify and filter inspired air, and their mucosa contains the trigeminal cold receptors that create the sensation of nasal patency. Aggressive or total resection can cause atrophic rhinitis with crusting, dryness and bleeding, and in some patients empty nose syndrome: a paradoxical sense of obstruction despite a wide airway. Mucosa-preserving submucosal reduction gives comparable relief of obstruction with much lower risk.', 'why_wrong': ['The Hasner valve can be injured with anterior inferior meatus surgery, but epiphora is uncommon and is not the main reason to preserve mucosa.', 'Septal perforation is a risk of septoplasty, not a long-term consequence of turbinate tissue removal.', 'Correct.', 'The inferior turbinate lies far from the olfactory cleft, so its resection does not cause anosmia.'], 'board_pearl': 'Total inferior turbinectomy risks atrophic rhinitis and empty nose syndrome; preserve mucosa.', 'curveball': 'Her examination also shows a septal deviation to the left. How does concomitant septal deviation affect surgical planning for her obstruction?', 'curveball_answer': 'Septal deviation often coexists with compensatory hypertrophy of the contralateral inferior turbinate, especially its bone. If the deviation contributes to obstruction, septoplasty is performed with turbinate reduction in the same sitting, and the concave-side turbinate is reduced so the straightened septum has room. Treating only one component leaves residual obstruction.'},
 'v208_rhi_turb_snr': {'stem': 'A 46-year-old man remains obstructed 1 year after septoplasty and conservative submucosal inferior turbinate reduction. Endoscopy shows a straight septum and small, patent turbinates. On quiet inspiration, both lateral nasal walls collapse inward at the internal valve, and breathing improves markedly when the lateral walls are supported with a cotton-tipped applicator. Which treatment is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Functional rhinoplasty with lateral wall cartilage grafts', 'Revision inferior turbinate reduction with partial bony resection', 'Repeat septoplasty for a residual posterior deviation', 'Radiofrequency reduction of the septal swell body'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Persistent obstruction after adequate septal and turbinate surgery requires reassessing the mechanism. Inspiratory lateral wall collapse that improves with lateral support identifies dynamic internal valve or lateral wall insufficiency. This is corrected by structural grafting, such as spreader, lateral crural strut or alar batten grafts, in a functional rhinoplasty. Removing more turbinate tissue does not stiffen the valve and risks crusting and empty nose symptoms.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'The turbinates are already small and patent; further reduction will not stop valve collapse and risks empty nose.', 'Endoscopy shows a straight septum, and posterior septal work will not affect inspiratory collapse at the valve.', 'The septal swell body can narrow the internal valve area, but it does not cause dynamic collapse of the lateral wall.'], 'board_pearl': 'Obstruction after septoturbinate surgery with inspiratory wall collapse: fix the valve with grafts, not more turbinate removal.', 'curveball': 'How would improvement with lateral wall support during examination influence functional rhinoplasty planning?', 'curveball_answer': 'Improvement when the lateral wall is supported at a specific point localizes the weakness. Support at the internal valve near the upper lateral cartilage favors spreader grafts or flaps, while support at the alar lobule or external valve favors lateral crural strut or alar batten grafts. A positive maneuver also predicts benefit and helps counsel the patient.'},
 'v136_rhi_17': {'stem': 'A 49-year-old woman with isolated right sphenoid sinusitis on CT is having office endoscopy to plan surgery. With a 0-degree scope, the resident passes medial to the middle turbinate along the septum toward the posterior nasal cavity and identifies the choana but cannot find the sphenoid ostium. The superior turbinate is visible above. Where is the natural sphenoid ostium most reliably found?', 'choices': ['Lateral to the middle turbinate, just posterior to the basal lamella', 'Within the retrobullar recess behind the ethmoid bulla', 'Directly above the posterior end of the inferior turbinate', 'Medial to the superior turbinate, about 1.5 cm above the choana'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'The sphenoid ostium opens into the sphenoethmoidal recess, medial to the superior turbinate and roughly 1 to 1.5 cm above the superior rim of the choana, close to the septum. The inferior third of the superior turbinate can be gently lateralized or resected to expose it. Using this fixed relationship keeps the surgeon low and medial, away from the skull base, optic nerve and carotid artery on the lateral sphenoid wall.', 'why_wrong': ['Behind the basal lamella lateral to the middle turbinate lie the posterior ethmoid cells, not the sphenoid ostium.', 'The retrobullar recess is an anterior ethmoid space that may lead to the frontal recess, not the sphenoethmoidal recess.', 'The area above the posterior inferior turbinate is near the sphenopalatine foramen region, which is lateral and lower than the ostium.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Sphenoid ostium: medial to the superior turbinate, about 1.5 cm above the choana, near the septum.', 'curveball': 'As the sphenoidotomy is enlarged inferiorly toward the choana, which major artery is at risk near the sphenopalatine foramen region?', 'curveball_answer': 'The sphenopalatine artery and its posterior septal branch, which crosses the anterior face of the sphenoid below the ostium on its way to the septum, are at risk. Enlarging the sphenoidotomy inferiorly can transect it, causing brisk bleeding and sacrificing the nasoseptal flap pedicle. Enlarge medially and superiorly, or cauterize and control the branch deliberately.'},
 'v144_rh_13': {'stem': 'A 35-year-old woman with recurrent right maxillary sinusitis is having diagnostic endoscopy with a 30-degree scope. Pus is draining over the inferior turbinate from the middle meatus, but no opening into the maxillary sinus is visible. The uncinate process, ethmoid bulla and middle turbinate are all intact. Behind which structure is the natural maxillary ostium hidden?', 'choices': ['The uncinate process, within the ethmoid infundibulum', 'The ethmoid bulla, within the retrobullar recess', 'The inferior turbinate, within the inferior meatus', 'The middle turbinate basal lamella, in the superior meatus'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'The natural maxillary ostium opens into the ethmoid infundibulum, the funnel-shaped space bounded medially by the uncinate process and laterally by the lamina papyracea. It lies at about the level of the inferior third of the uncinate and is usually not visible on routine endoscopy until the uncinate is removed. Accessory ostia in the posterior fontanelle are often visible and mistaken for the natural one. Knowing this relationship is the basis of a functional antrostomy.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'The retrobullar recess lies behind the bulla and may communicate with the frontal recess, but the maxillary ostium is anterior to it.', 'The inferior meatus receives the nasolacrimal duct; the natural maxillary ostium does not open there.', 'The superior meatus drains posterior ethmoid cells, not the maxillary sinus.'], 'board_pearl': 'The natural maxillary ostium is hidden behind the uncinate in the infundibulum; visible openings are often accessory.', 'curveball': 'After the uncinate is removed, an accessory ostium is also seen posteriorly. Why can connecting the natural and accessory ostia matter?', 'curveball_answer': 'Mucociliary flow carries mucus to the natural ostium. If a separate accessory ostium remains, mucus leaving the natural ostium can be swept back into the sinus through the accessory one, creating a recirculation loop that maintains infection. Joining the two into one antrostomy breaks the circuit and is a key principle of functional maxillary surgery.'},
 'v208_rhi_anat_snr': {'stem': 'During revision bilateral ESS in a 54-year-old man with prior ethmoidectomy and middle turbinate resection elsewhere, the resident is working high in the right ethmoid cavity. Scar has obliterated the usual landmarks, and a thin horizontal partition above is either a residual cell wall or skull base. Image guidance registration was not checked after repositioning the head. What is the safest next move?', 'choices': ['Remove the partition with through-cutting forceps to see above it', 'Continue superiorly with the microdebrider while watching the eye', 'Stop and relocalize with fixed landmarks, CT and rechecked navigation', 'Abandon the operation now and refer the patient to a tertiary rhinologist'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Revision surgery removes many landmarks used in primary ESS, and the middle turbinate, which normally marks the medial limit near the skull base, is gone. When orientation is lost near the orbit or skull base, the right response is to stop and deliberately relocalize. Use fixed landmarks such as the maxillary sinus roof, lamina papyracea, choana and sphenoid face, correlate them with multiplanar CT, and verify navigation accuracy on known points before proceeding.', 'why_wrong': ['Taking an unidentified partition is how skull base injuries happen; through-cutting forceps reduce tearing but do not confirm identity.', 'Powered dissection near uncertain skull base risks rapid CSF leak or brain injury, and watching the eye does not protect the roof.', 'Correct.', 'Abandoning is premature; most disorientation can be resolved safely by stopping, relocalizing and verifying navigation.'], 'board_pearl': 'Lost orientation in revision ESS: stop, relocalize from fixed landmarks and CT, and recheck navigation before proceeding.', 'curveball': 'Which fixed landmarks remain especially useful after prior uncinectomy, ethmoidectomy and middle turbinate resection?', 'curveball_answer': 'The roof of the maxillary sinus (orbital floor) approximates the height of the posterior ethmoid roof and serves as a safe vertical reference. The medial orbital wall, the choana, the posterior septum and the sphenoid ostium near the septum are unaffected by prior ethmoid surgery. The skull base is often identified in the sphenoid first and followed forward.'},
 'v136_rhi_18': {'stem': "A 31-year-old man reports severe right nasal obstruction for 2 years, rating it 85 on the NOSE scale. Anterior rhinoscopy shows only a mild right septal deflection, normal-appearing turbinates and no visible valve collapse. He has not used any nasal medication. He asks for septoplasty because he 'cannot breathe on the right.' What is the best next step in his evaluation?", 'choices': ['Sinus CT to measure the right airway cross-sectional area', 'Endoscopy before and after decongestion, adding airflow testing as needed', 'Septoplasty now, since his symptom severity outweighs the mild deviation seen', 'Reassurance, because a mild deflection cannot cause severe symptoms'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Nasal obstruction is multidimensional, and anterior rhinoscopy alone misses posterior deviations, adenoid tissue, polyps, masses and dynamic valve collapse. Endoscopy before and after topical decongestion separates mucosal from fixed structural narrowing. Validated symptom scores such as NOSE quantify burden, and acoustic rhinometry or rhinomanometry can be helpful adjuncts, while recognizing that measured airflow and perceived obstruction correlate only modestly. A medication trial may also be appropriate before surgery.', 'why_wrong': ['CT is not a validated measure of nasal airflow and exposes him to radiation without addressing mucosal or dynamic causes.', 'Correct.', 'Operating before a complete examination risks missing the true cause and leaving him obstructed after surgery.', 'Severe symptoms with a mild anterior finding call for a fuller evaluation, not reassurance; posterior or dynamic causes may be present.'], 'board_pearl': 'Obstruction out of proportion to anterior rhinoscopy: perform endoscopy with decongestion; symptoms and airflow tests correlate only modestly.', 'curveball': 'How would his response to topical decongestant help distinguish mucosal from fixed structural obstruction?', 'curveball_answer': 'If obstruction and the visible narrowing resolve after oxymetazoline, the cause is reversible mucosal engorgement, pointing toward rhinitis treatment or turbinate reduction. Persistent narrowing after decongestion indicates a fixed septal, bony or valve obstruction. On acoustic rhinometry, a minimal cross-sectional area that does not increase after decongestion supports a structural cause.'},
 'v144_rh_15': {'stem': 'A 44-year-old woman has bilateral nasal obstruction with a NOSE score of 70. Examination shows a moderate caudal septal deviation and mild turbinate hypertrophy that improves with decongestion. Her insurer will cover septoplasty only if surgery is medically justified, and her surgeon obtains rhinomanometry, which shows moderately elevated resistance on the left. How should this rhinomanometry result be used?', 'choices': ['As the test that sets a firm threshold for whether she needs septoplasty', 'As a complement to her symptoms and examination when weighing surgery', 'As proof that allergic rhinitis is the main cause of her obstruction', 'As a replacement for endoscopy when anterior examination is equivocal'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Rhinomanometry measures transnasal pressure and airflow to calculate resistance, and acoustic rhinometry maps cross-sectional area along the nose. Both can document and quantify obstruction, especially for comparison before and after decongestion or surgery. However, perceived obstruction depends also on mucosal sensation and dynamic collapse, so test results correlate only modestly with symptoms and have no universal surgical threshold. They complement history, examination and endoscopy rather than replacing them.', 'why_wrong': ['No validated rhinomanometry threshold reliably predicts who will benefit from septoplasty.', 'Correct.', 'Rhinomanometry measures resistance, not etiology; allergy requires history and skin or specific IgE testing.', 'Rhinomanometry gives no anatomic information about posterior disease or masses, which only endoscopy can show.'], 'board_pearl': 'Objective nasal tests quantify resistance or area but do not override clinical judgment; correlation with symptoms is modest.', 'curveball': 'Why can the Cottle maneuver overpredict benefit from nasal valve surgery in patients like her?', 'curveball_answer': 'Lateral cheek traction opens the internal valve but also widens the vestibule and alters the external valve and even septal relationships, so it improves airflow in many people without valve disease. A positive Cottle therefore has poor specificity. A modified Cottle, supporting the lateral wall at a specific point with a curette, better localizes the weak segment and predicts graft benefit.'},
 'v208_rhi_objective_snr': {'stem': 'A 52-year-old woman has a NOSE score of 90 and severe bilateral obstruction worse during exercise. Endoscopy shows a straight septum and normal turbinates. On deep inspiration both lateral walls collapse, and supporting the upper lateral cartilages with a curette dramatically relieves her symptoms. Resting rhinomanometry is only mildly abnormal. Her prior surgeon declined surgery because of the test. What is the best interpretation?', 'choices': ['Offer valve surgery, as concordant dynamic findings outweigh one mild test', 'Decline surgery, because her resting rhinomanometry is only mildly abnormal', 'Refer for psychological evaluation of a mismatch between symptoms and testing', 'Repeat rhinomanometry after decongestion and operate only if it doubles'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Perceived obstruction reflects resistance, geometry, mucosal sensation and dynamic collapse. Resting rhinomanometry measures resistance at quiet breathing and often misses collapse that occurs only at higher inspiratory flow, such as during exercise. Here the history, observed inspiratory collapse and marked relief with targeted lateral wall support form a concordant mechanism. Objective tests are adjuncts with no universal surgical threshold, and a single mildly abnormal value should not overrule a coherent clinical diagnosis.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'A resting test can underestimate dynamic collapse and does not have a validated threshold that should veto surgery.', 'Her symptoms are explained by visible inspiratory collapse, so labeling the mismatch as psychological is unwarranted.', 'Decongestion affects mucosa, not cartilage support, and no validated doubling threshold exists to guide surgery.'], 'board_pearl': 'Dynamic valve collapse may be missed by resting rhinomanometry; a concordant exam drives the surgical decision.', 'curveball': 'Why can a nonspecific Cottle maneuver overestimate benefit compared with targeted lateral wall support?', 'curveball_answer': 'Pulling the cheek laterally opens the internal valve, vestibule and external valve all at once, and it helps airflow in many people without valve disease. Targeted support with a curette at the upper lateral cartilage or alar rim tests one segment, localizing which region collapses and which graft, such as spreader, batten or lateral crural strut, will help.'},
 'v136_rhi_19': {'stem': 'A 51-year-old man has 10 weeks of right-sided foul postnasal drainage and cacosmia after two antibiotic courses. Endoscopy shows pus from the right middle meatus. CT shows right maxillary opacification with patent ethmoid and frontal sinuses and a large periapical lucency at the right maxillary first molar root that breaches the sinus floor. The tooth is mildly tender. What is the best management?', 'choices': ['Six weeks of amoxicillin-clavulanate, then repeat CT', 'ESS with maxillary antrostomy, deferring dental care', 'Coordinated dental treatment of the tooth, adding ESS if needed', 'Intranasal steroid and saline irrigation for presumed chronic rhinosinusitis'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Unilateral maxillary disease with foul drainage and a periapical lesion breaching the sinus floor is odontogenic sinusitis. Repeated antibiotics cannot control an untreated dental focus, and sinus surgery alone leaves the source behind. Durable cure requires dental evaluation and treatment, such as endodontic therapy or extraction, coordinated with ENT. Endoscopic sinus surgery is added when sinus disease persists after dental treatment or when severe symptoms or spread warrant earlier drainage; sequencing is individualized.', 'why_wrong': ['More antibiotics have already failed and do not control the infected tooth that keeps seeding the sinus.', 'Sinus surgery without source control commonly fails because the infected tooth continues to reinfect the maxillary sinus.', 'Correct.', 'Topical therapy treats inflammatory CRS, but this is a unilateral infection with a clear dental source.'], 'board_pearl': 'Unilateral foul maxillary sinusitis with periapical disease: odontogenic; treat the tooth and the sinus together.', 'curveball': 'His dentist extracts the molar, and fluid now leaks from his mouth into his nose when he drinks. How does an oroantral fistula change management?', 'curveball_answer': 'A persistent oroantral fistula keeps contaminating the sinus and will not close while the sinus is infected. Clear the sinus infection, often with endoscopic maxillary antrostomy, and close the fistula, typically with a buccal advancement or buccal fat pad flap, in the same or a staged procedure. Small fresh communications under about 2 mm may close spontaneously.'},
 'v143_rhi_05': {'stem': 'A 60-year-old woman had an abscessed left maxillary second molar extracted 3 months ago, and the socket has healed without a fistula. She still has left facial pressure, nasal obstruction and foul drainage despite a 3-week antibiotic course. CT shows persistent left maxillary, anterior ethmoid and frontal opacification with an obstructed ostiomeatal complex. Her dentist finds no remaining dental pathology. What is the best next step?', 'choices': ['Endoscopic sinus surgery to open the obstructed left sinuses', 'A further 6 weeks of antibiotics until the CT has cleared', 'Extraction of the adjacent premolar as a possible second source', 'Caldwell-Luc approach with removal of the maxillary mucosa'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Odontogenic sinusitis requires control of both the dental source and the diseased sinus. Here the dental source has been eliminated, but infection has spread beyond the maxillary sinus to obstruct the ostiomeatal complex, ethmoid and frontal sinuses. Persistent disease after source control is an indication for ESS to restore drainage of the involved sinuses. Treating the dental and sinus components in coordination is what produces durable resolution.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Prolonged antibiotics cannot open an obstructed ostiomeatal complex that is keeping infected sinuses closed.', 'The dentist found no remaining pathology, and removing a healthy tooth adds harm without treating the obstructed sinuses.', 'Radical mucosal removal is outdated and unnecessary; endoscopic drainage restores function with less morbidity.'], 'board_pearl': 'Persistent sinus obstruction after dental source control in odontogenic sinusitis is an indication for ESS.', 'curveball': 'Suppose the extraction socket had instead left an oroantral fistula. How would that change the surgical plan?', 'curveball_answer': 'The fistula must be closed, and repair is most successful once the sinus infection is cleared. Plan ESS to drain the maxillary and other involved sinuses together with, or before, fistula closure by an oral surgeon or ENT using a buccal advancement or buccal fat pad flap. Repairing the fistula over an infected sinus has a high failure rate.'},
 'v213_rhi_odont_found': {'stem': 'A 46-year-old man has 3 months of right facial pressure and foul-smelling drainage that he can taste. He had a root canal on a right upper molar a year ago. Endoscopy shows purulence in the right middle meatus. CT shows right maxillary opacification centered on the floor of the sinus around a molar root with a periapical lucency, without calcifications or bone erosion. Which diagnosis best fits?', 'choices': ['Maxillary sinus fungal ball', 'Inverted papilloma of the lateral wall', 'Odontogenic maxillary sinusitis', 'Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Odontogenic sinusitis should be suspected in unilateral maxillary-predominant disease with foul drainage or cacosmia, particularly with dental symptoms, prior dental procedures, periapical disease or an oroantral communication. The 2021 consensus requires ENT confirmation of sinusitis plus dental confirmation of the causal pathology. Anaerobes are common, producing the foul smell. The deciding clue is opacification centered on a molar with a periapical lucency after endodontic treatment.', 'why_wrong': ['A fungal ball typically shows central hyperdensities or calcifications, which are absent here.', 'Inverted papilloma is a lateral wall mass, often with focal hyperostosis at the attachment, not centered on a diseased tooth.', 'Correct.', 'AFRS is usually multisinus, with expansion and heterogeneous hyperdense content in atopic patients, not centered on a molar.'], 'board_pearl': 'Unilateral foul maxillary disease plus dental pathology on CT: think odontogenic sinusitis and confirm with dentistry.', 'curveball': 'Which dental procedures can create an oroantral communication and change source-control planning?', 'curveball_answer': 'Extraction of maxillary molars or premolars whose roots project into the sinus, dental implant placement, sinus floor augmentation (sinus lift) with graft material, and endodontic treatment with overextended filling material can all breach the sinus floor. Displaced implants, graft particles or roots act as foreign bodies that must be removed and the communication closed.'},
 'v213_rhi_odont_snr': {'stem': 'A 57-year-old woman had a left maxillary first molar extracted 4 months ago. She has recurrent foul left nasal drainage, and fluids pass from her mouth into her nose. Each antibiotic course briefly helps. A 6-mm oroantral fistula is present at the socket, and CT shows left maxillary and anterior ethmoid opacification with an obstructed ostiomeatal complex. What is the best management plan?', 'choices': ['Suppressive antibiotics until the fistula closes spontaneously', 'Primary buccal flap closure of the fistula, deferring all sinus treatment', 'Coordinated fistula closure with ESS to drain the obstructed sinuses', 'ESS alone, leaving the fistula to heal by secondary intention'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Durable treatment requires eliminating the source and draining the infected sinuses. A 6-mm fistula present for 4 months is epithelialized and will not close spontaneously, and closure over an infected, obstructed sinus fails often. The standard approach is ESS to open the maxillary sinus and ostiomeatal complex, combined in one stage or staged with fistula closure using a buccal advancement or buccal fat pad flap. Sequencing depends on expertise and disease extent.', 'why_wrong': ['A chronic, epithelialized fistula of this size will not close with antibiotics, and recurrence will continue.', 'Closing the fistula while the sinus remains infected and obstructed has a high failure rate.', 'Correct.', 'Leaving an epithelialized fistula open allows ongoing oral contamination of the sinus, so ESS alone will fail.'], 'board_pearl': 'Chronic oroantral fistula with sinusitis: drain the sinus endoscopically and close the fistula, together or staged.', 'curveball': 'In another patient a displaced dental implant sits within the maxillary sinus. How does that alter the operative plan?', 'curveball_answer': 'The implant is a foreign body that must be removed, usually through a wide endoscopic middle meatal antrostomy, sometimes adding a prelacrimal or canine fossa approach if it lies anteriorly or inferiorly. Any associated oroantral communication is closed in coordination with the oral surgeon, and future implant placement is deferred until the sinus has healed.'},
 'v136_rhi_20': {'stem': 'A 43-year-old woman lost her sense of smell during an upper respiratory infection 4 months ago and it has not returned. She has no nasal obstruction, rhinorrhea, head trauma or neurologic symptoms. Endoscopy shows open olfactory clefts with healthy mucosa, and a validated smell test confirms hyposmia. She asks what treatment offers the best balance of benefit and risk. What do you recommend?', 'choices': ['Olfactory training twice daily for several months', 'Oral zinc sulfate supplementation daily for 3 months', 'A 3-week tapering course of oral prednisone', 'Endoscopic surgery to widen the olfactory clefts'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Postviral olfactory loss is sensorineural, caused by damage to olfactory neuroepithelium, and many patients recover partially over months to years. Olfactory training, repeated structured sniffing of several distinct odors, is low risk, inexpensive, and supported by consensus guidance including ICAR-Olfaction as the first-line therapy. Systemic steroids have limited evidence and real toxicity in postviral loss, and zinc has no proven benefit. The normal endoscopy is the deciding clue that nothing needs to be opened.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Zinc has not shown benefit for postviral olfactory loss and is not recommended.', 'Oral steroids have weak evidence in postviral loss and carry systemic risk; they suit inflammatory or polyp-related loss better.', 'Her olfactory clefts are already open and healthy, so surgery has no conductive target to correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Postviral smell loss with normal endoscopy: olfactory training is the first-line, low-risk treatment.', 'curveball': 'What unilateral nasal or neurologic findings would prompt imaging in a patient with smell loss?', 'curveball_answer': 'Unilateral smell loss, unilateral nasal obstruction, epistaxis or a mass on endoscopy suggests a sinonasal tumor such as olfactory neuroblastoma, and needs CT and MRI. Headache, visual change, seizures, personality change or other cranial neuropathies suggest an intracranial lesion such as an olfactory groove meningioma, and warrant contrast MRI.'},
 'v144_rh_16': {'stem': 'A 36-year-old man has persistent anosmia 6 months after a viral illness. Endoscopy shows open, healthy olfactory clefts, and a validated psychophysical smell test confirms anosmia. He has no obstruction or neurologic symptoms. He agrees to start olfactory training and asks exactly what it involves. Which regimen best describes standard olfactory training?', 'choices': ['Smelling one strong odor continuously for an hour each day for a month', 'Sniffing any household odors once weekly until smell begins to return', 'Sniffing four distinct odors for about 20 seconds each, twice daily for months', 'Inhaling menthol vapor several times daily to stimulate trigeminal nerve endings'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Olfactory training uses repeated, structured exposure to several distinct odorants, classically rose, eucalyptus, lemon and clove, sniffed for about 10 to 20 seconds each, twice daily, for at least 12 weeks and often longer. The patient concentrates on each odor and its memory. It is thought to promote regeneration and central reorganization of the olfactory pathway. It is low risk and is recommended for postviral and other sensorineural losses, with retesting to track progress.', 'why_wrong': ['Prolonged exposure to a single odor promotes adaptation rather than training and lacks the variety that defines the protocol.', 'Infrequent, unstructured exposure is not the twice-daily regimen shown to help recovery.', 'Correct.', 'Menthol stimulates trigeminal cold receptors, not olfactory neurons, and does not retrain the olfactory pathway.'], 'board_pearl': 'Olfactory training: four odors, about 20 seconds each, twice daily, for at least 12 weeks.', 'curveball': 'Which unilateral smell-loss or neurologic features should trigger imaging or broader evaluation in this patient?', 'curveball_answer': 'Smell loss on one side only, unilateral obstruction, bleeding or a mass suggests a sinonasal or olfactory groove tumor and needs CT and contrast MRI. New headache, seizures, visual loss, personality change or cognitive decline suggest an intracranial or neurodegenerative process. Anosmia without any viral trigger in an older adult also warrants broader evaluation.'},
 'v208_rhi_smell_snr': {'stem': "A 48-year-old woman has had anosmia for 14 months after COVID-19. Endoscopy and sinus CT are normal, with clear olfactory clefts, and she has no unilateral or neurologic features. She has done olfactory training inconsistently. She read that sinus surgery can 'open up' the smell area and asks to schedule it. What is the best counseling decision?", 'choices': ['Offer ESS to open the olfactory clefts as a trial for recovery', 'Order a brain MRI before any further discussion of treatment', 'Decline surgery, renew training, and counsel on household safety', 'Advise avoiding strong odor exposure so the neuroepithelium can rest'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Post-COVID olfactory dysfunction is primarily sensorineural when the airway and olfactory clefts are unobstructed, so ESS has no anatomic target and cannot restore smell. Senior counseling explains prognosis, recommits her to consistent olfactory training, and addresses safety: smoke and natural gas detectors, food expiration dates and caution with cooking. Imaging is reserved for atypical, unilateral or neurologic features, which she lacks, and new red flags would prompt it.', 'why_wrong': ['Her olfactory clefts and sinuses are normal, so surgery exposes her to risk without any obstruction to relieve.', 'Imaging is not needed for typical postviral loss with a normal exam and no red flags.', 'Correct.', 'Avoiding odors deprives the olfactory system of the stimulation that training uses to aid recovery.'], 'board_pearl': 'Sensorineural smell loss has no surgical target; counsel, train and protect from gas, smoke and spoiled food.', 'curveball': 'What household safety counseling is important for patients who cannot reliably detect smoke, gas or spoiled food?', 'curveball_answer': 'Install and test smoke detectors and natural gas or carbon monoxide detectors on every level, consider switching gas appliances to electric, label and date food and discard it by date rather than by smell, and have someone else check for spoilage. Avoid mixing household chemicals, and ask others to check for body odor if this concerns the patient.'},
 'v136_rhi_21': {'stem': 'A 5-year-old boy has 5 months of daily purulent rhinorrhea, nasal obstruction, mouth breathing and night cough despite 3 weeks of antibiotics, saline irrigation and intranasal steroid. He has no asthma, failure to thrive, recurrent pneumonia or neonatal respiratory problems. Lateral neck radiograph shows large adenoids, and CT shows moderate bilateral maxillary and ethmoid disease. What is the most appropriate first surgical step?', 'choices': ['Bilateral maxillary antrostomy with anterior ethmoidectomy', 'Adenoidectomy, with or without maxillary sinus irrigation', 'Six weeks of IV antibiotics through a central line', 'Inferior turbinate reduction with adenoid preservation'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'In young children the adenoid acts as both a bacterial reservoir, harboring biofilm, and an obstruction to nasopharyngeal drainage. The pediatric CRS consensus statement supports adenoidectomy as the first surgical intervention for medically refractory CRS in children up to about age 12, with maxillary irrigation or balloon dilation sometimes added. Endoscopic sinus surgery is reserved for persistent disease after adenoidectomy. His lack of systemic or lower airway red flags makes CF, PCD or immunodeficiency less likely.', 'why_wrong': ['ESS is effective but is generally reserved for children whose CRS persists after adenoidectomy.', 'Correct.', 'IV antibiotics carry line-related risk and are not a standard first escalation in uncomplicated pediatric CRS.', 'Turbinate reduction does not address the adenoid reservoir and nasopharyngeal obstruction driving his disease.'], 'board_pearl': 'Medically refractory pediatric CRS: adenoidectomy first, with ESS for persistent disease.', 'curveball': 'What history would make CF or PCD evaluation important before escalating surgery in a child like him?', 'curveball_answer': 'Nasal polyps in any child should prompt sweat chloride testing for CF, as should failure to thrive, malabsorption or Pseudomonas. Neonatal respiratory distress in a term infant, year-round wet cough from infancy, chronic otitis with persistent otorrhea, situs inversus or bronchiectasis suggest PCD and warrant nasal nitric oxide and ciliary or genetic testing.'},
 'v144_rh_17': {'stem': 'A 6-year-old girl has 4 months of nasal obstruction, purulent drainage, snoring and chronic cough despite appropriate antibiotics, saline and topical steroid. Endoscopy shows enlarged adenoids with purulence in the nasopharynx. CT shows bilateral maxillary and ethmoid mucosal disease. She has no orbital or intracranial complications. Her parents ask if she needs the same sinus surgery her father had. What is the best advice?', 'choices': ['Full ESS now, since CT confirms multisinus disease', 'Continue antibiotics until her CT has completely cleared', 'Defer any surgery until age 12, when the sinuses have matured', 'Adenoidectomy first, with sinus surgery if disease persists'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'For children with CRS refractory to medical therapy, adenoidectomy is commonly the first surgical step because the adenoid serves as a bacterial reservoir and nasopharyngeal obstruction. It is less invasive than ESS and improves symptoms in many children. ESS, tailored to the involved sinuses and preserving mucosa, is reserved for disease that persists after adenoidectomy or for complications. Concerns that ESS impairs facial growth have not been substantiated, so surgery need not be delayed to a set age.', 'why_wrong': ['CT disease is common in children, and full ESS is generally reserved for failure of adenoidectomy.', 'CT findings often persist after symptom improvement, and prolonged antibiotics without source control add resistance and side effects.', 'Waiting years leaves her symptomatic; adenoidectomy and, if needed, ESS are safe in young children.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'In refractory pediatric CRS, adenoidectomy comes before ESS; ESS has not been shown to impair facial growth.', 'curveball': 'Which comorbidities should lower your threshold to evaluate a child with CRS for CF, PCD or immune deficiency?', 'curveball_answer': 'Nasal polyps, failure to thrive, steatorrhea or Pseudomonas suggest CF and warrant sweat chloride. Neonatal respiratory distress, persistent wet cough, chronic otorrhea and situs abnormalities suggest PCD. Recurrent pneumonias, otitis, invasive bacterial infection or infections that fail standard therapy suggest an antibody deficiency and warrant immunoglobulin levels and vaccine titers.'},
 'v208_rhi_pedscrs_snr': {'stem': 'A 7-year-old boy has persistent CRS 1 year after adenoidectomy with maxillary irrigation. He had respiratory distress at birth despite being born at term, has had a daily wet cough since infancy, needed three sets of ear tubes for persistent otorrhea, and chest CT shows bronchiectasis. Sweat chloride is normal. CT shows diffuse sinus opacification. What should happen next?', 'choices': ['Nasal nitric oxide and ciliary or genetic testing for PCD', 'Complete ESS with bilateral frontal sinusotomies now', 'Quantitative immunoglobulins as the only further test needed now', 'Revision adenoidectomy to remove any adenoid regrowth'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Neonatal respiratory distress in a term infant, year-round wet cough from infancy, chronic otorrhea, bronchiectasis and refractory CRS form the classic phenotype of primary ciliary dyskinesia. A normal sweat chloride makes CF less likely. Low nasal nitric oxide is a screening test, with confirmation by high-speed video microscopy, electron microscopy or genetic panels. Diagnosis enables coordinated airway clearance and pulmonary care, while any sinus surgery is tailored to a defined goal because surgery cannot correct the ciliary defect.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Surgery may help drainage and topical access but should follow diagnosis, since it does not correct the systemic clearance defect.', 'Immunoglobulins are reasonable to check, but his phenotype points specifically to a ciliary disorder that needs its own testing.', 'Adenoid regrowth does not explain lifelong lower airway disease, neonatal distress and bronchiectasis.'], 'board_pearl': 'Neonatal distress, lifelong wet cough, chronic otorrhea and bronchiectasis with CRS: test for PCD with nasal nitric oxide.', 'curveball': 'Which laterality or fertility findings can further support a primary ciliary dyskinesia phenotype?', 'curveball_answer': 'About half of patients with PCD have situs inversus totalis (Kartagener syndrome), and others have heterotaxy with congenital heart disease or asplenia or polysplenia. In adults, male infertility from immotile sperm and reduced female fertility or ectopic pregnancy from impaired tubal cilia support the diagnosis. A family history of similar disease or consanguinity also raises suspicion.'},
 'v136_rhi_22': {'stem': 'A 34-year-old woman has had four episodes of facial pain and purulent drainage in the past year, each lasting about 2 weeks and documented by her physician with endoscopic purulence. A CT obtained during one episode showed maxillary air-fluid levels. Between episodes she is completely well, and endoscopy today, 6 weeks after the last one, is normal. Which feature most distinguishes her condition from CRS?', 'choices': ['Sinus opacification on CT during her episodes', 'Facial pressure accompanying each of the episodes', 'Prompt response to antibiotics with each episode', 'Complete return to baseline between the episodes'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Recurrent acute rhinosinusitis is defined as four or more episodes of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis per year without persistent symptoms between them. CRS, by contrast, requires at least 12 weeks of continuous symptoms with objective inflammation on endoscopy or CT. Imaging abnormalities, facial pressure and antibiotic response occur in both. The deciding clue is her symptom-free intervals with a normal endoscopy between episodes, which places her in the recurrent acute category.', 'why_wrong': ['CT opacification occurs in both acute and chronic disease, so it does not separate recurrent acute from chronic rhinosinusitis.', 'Facial pressure is a cardinal symptom of both acute and chronic rhinosinusitis and does not distinguish them.', 'Antibiotic response is common in acute episodes and in CRS exacerbations, so it is not a defining feature.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'RARS: four or more ABRS episodes per year with complete resolution in between; CRS: 12 weeks of continuous disease.', 'curveball': 'She is being considered for surgery. When should CT be timed to maximize diagnostic value in recurrent acute disease?', 'curveball_answer': 'A CT during an acute episode documents that the episodes are truly sinusitis, showing opacification or air-fluid levels, which is especially useful when diagnosis is uncertain. A CT between episodes is used for surgical planning, showing anatomic contributors such as a narrow infundibulum, Haller cell or septal deviation. Ideally both are available; a normal interval CT alone does not confirm the diagnosis.'},
 'v144_rh_18': {'stem': 'A 29-year-old man reports five sinus infections in the past year. Records confirm each began after a cold, worsened after initial improvement, and had endoscopically documented purulence that cleared after amoxicillin. Between episodes he has no congestion, drainage or smell loss, and interval endoscopy and CT are normal. He has seasonal allergies controlled with a nasal steroid. What diagnosis best fits?', 'choices': ['Chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps', 'Recurrent acute rhinosinusitis', 'Acute exacerbations of chronic rhinosinusitis', 'Persistent allergic rhinitis with viral infections'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Recurrent acute rhinosinusitis consists of four or more discrete episodes of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis per year with full resolution between them. His episodes show the classic bacterial pattern of double worsening with documented purulence, and his interval endoscopy and CT are normal. CRS requires at least 12 weeks of continuous symptoms with objective inflammation, and acute exacerbations of CRS occur on a background of persistent disease, which he lacks.', 'why_wrong': ['CRS requires continuous symptoms for 12 weeks with objective findings, but he is symptom-free with normal interval imaging.', 'Correct.', 'Acute exacerbations of CRS are flares superimposed on persistent baseline disease, which he does not have.', 'Viral colds with allergy do not produce documented purulence with double worsening that clears after antibiotics.'], 'board_pearl': 'Double-worsening episodes with documented purulence and normal intervals, four or more a year: RARS.', 'curveball': 'His episodes continue despite allergy control and a nasal steroid. What management options does the adult sinusitis guideline support?', 'curveball_answer': 'Evaluate modifying factors, including allergy, immunodeficiency, and anatomic narrowing on CT, and treat each documented episode appropriately. For persistent RARS with disease confirmed during episodes and anatomic contributors on interval CT, endoscopic sinus surgery is a reasonable option through shared decision-making. Balloon dilation of the involved ostia is an alternative in selected patients. Long-term prophylactic antibiotics are not recommended.'},
 'v209_rhi_rars_snr': {'stem': "A 39-year-old woman requests sinus surgery for five 'sinus infections' a year. Her records show most episodes lasted 3 to 5 days, improved without antibiotics, and had clear rhinorrhea with no documented purulence. Two episodes coincided with throbbing headache and nausea. Endoscopy and CT obtained between episodes are normal. She asks to schedule surgery now. What is the best decision?", 'choices': ['Schedule ESS because five episodes yearly meet the surgical threshold', 'Prescribe prophylactic azithromycin through the winter months', 'Treat as CRS with continuous topical steroid and repeat cultures', 'Document future episodes with endoscopy or CT before offering surgery'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'The label recurrent acute rhinosinusitis should be reserved for distinct episodes that behave like acute bacterial rhinosinusitis. Her short self-limited episodes without purulence fit viral colds, and some have migrainous features. A self-reported count without objective support does not justify an operation. The next step is to verify the diagnosis by examining her during an episode, with endoscopy showing purulence or CT showing disease, and to evaluate mimics such as migraine, allergic rhinitis and viral infections.', 'why_wrong': ['Episode counts mean little without evidence that episodes are bacterial; operating risks treating viral colds or migraine.', 'Prophylactic antibiotics are not recommended for RARS and are inappropriate when bacterial episodes are unproven.', 'She has normal interval endoscopy and CT, so CRS criteria are not met and cultures have no target.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Verify RARS objectively during episodes before surgery; short, nonpurulent episodes are usually viral or migraine.', 'curveball': 'When is a CT during an acute episode most useful in a patient being considered for surgery for RARS?', 'curveball_answer': 'When the diagnosis is uncertain, as in her case, or when endoscopy during an episode is unavailable or inconclusive. A CT during symptoms showing sinus opacification or air-fluid levels confirms that the episodes are sinus disease. A normal CT during symptoms argues strongly against rhinosinusitis and redirects evaluation toward migraine, rhinitis or other mimics before any surgery.'},
 'v136_rhi_23': {'stem': "A 46-year-old man has persistent nasal obstruction, drainage and facial pressure 1 year after endoscopic sinus surgery at another hospital. He uses his saline rinses only occasionally and stopped his nasal steroid. He has asthma. He brings an operative note describing 'bilateral FESS' without detail and requests revision surgery as soon as possible. What is the most important next step before scheduling revision?", 'choices': ['Book revision ESS with image guidance at the earliest date', 'Prescribe a 6-week course of culture-directed antibiotics', 'Plan a more aggressive version of the original operation', 'Define the failure mechanism with endoscopy, CT and records'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Persistent symptoms after ESS do not automatically mean surgical failure, and revision should be hypothesis-driven. Endoscopy, fine-cut CT and the original operative details identify anatomic causes such as residual uncinate or cells, recirculation, middle turbinate lateralization and frontal stenosis. Equally important are inflammatory phenotype, here suggested by asthma, poor adherence to topical therapy, odontogenic sources, immune deficiency and nonrhinogenic symptoms. Each explanation calls for a different solution.', 'why_wrong': ['Scheduling before understanding why he failed risks repeating the problem and ignores his poor medication adherence.', 'Prolonged antibiotics have little role in CRS without a documented infection and do not define the failure mechanism.', 'More aggressive surgery without a defined target adds risk and may not address inflammation or adherence.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Revision ESS must be hypothesis-driven: find the anatomic, inflammatory or adherence reason for failure first.', 'curveball': 'On review of his CT and endoscopy, what finding suggests maxillary recirculation?', 'curveball_answer': 'Endoscopy shows mucus exiting the natural ostium, which is still hidden behind a residual uncinate, and re-entering the sinus through a separate posterior antrostomy. On CT, a residual uncinate with an intact infundibulum and a separate surgical opening in the posterior fontanelle, without a connection between them, supports the diagnosis.'},
 'v142_rhi_04': {'stem': 'A 51-year-old woman with recurrent CRSwNP is scheduled for revision bilateral ESS after two prior operations elsewhere. Endoscopy shows polyps, scar bands and a partially resected left middle turbinate. Fine-cut CT shows residual frontal cells, a dehiscent left lamina papyracea and an asymmetric skull base. Image guidance will be used. Which planning step most reduces her operative risk?', 'choices': ['Rely on intraoperative navigation instead of a detailed preoperative CT review', 'Use the remnant middle turbinate as the main landmark for skull base height', 'Map residual cells, skull base, orbit and frontal pathway on triplanar CT', 'Start in the posterior ethmoid, where revision scar is usually the thinnest'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Revision surgery is riskier because landmarks may be missing, displaced or scarred, and here the lamina is dehiscent and the skull base asymmetric. Systematic review of thin-cut CT in axial, coronal and sagittal planes before surgery identifies residual partitions, the frontal drainage pathway, orbital dehiscence and skull base height on each side. This creates a landmark-based plan that works from known to unknown. Navigation supplements anatomy but has registration error and does not replace this preparation.', 'why_wrong': ["Navigation can drift and is an adjunct; it does not replace a surgeon's understanding of the patient's anatomy.", 'A partially resected middle turbinate is unreliable, and using it as a skull base reference can mislead the surgeon.', 'Correct.', 'Scar thickness is not predictable, and starting posteriorly without a plan puts the skull base and orbit at risk.'], 'board_pearl': 'Revision ESS: study triplanar CT for residual cells, dehiscences and skull base height; navigation is an adjunct.', 'curveball': 'Which stable landmarks are especially useful when the middle turbinate has been partially resected?', 'curveball_answer': 'The maxillary sinus roof marks the orbital floor and approximates the posterior ethmoid roof height. The lamina papyracea, the posterior choana and nasal floor, the sphenoid ostium medial to the superior turbinate, and the remnant basal lamella or vertical attachment of the middle turbinate remain helpful. The skull base is often best identified in the sphenoid and followed forward.'},
 'v209_rhi_revision_snr': {'stem': 'A 55-year-old man has persistent right foul drainage after ESS 18 months ago. Endoscopy shows a widely patent right maxillary antrostomy that includes the natural ostium and a healed ethmoid cavity, with pus pooling on the maxillary floor. CT shows maxillary floor thickening around a molar root with a large periapical lucency, and the operative report confirms complete uncinectomy. What is the best revision principle?', 'choices': ['Dental treatment of the molar without revising the patent sinuses', 'Revision ESS with a wider modified endoscopic medial maxillectomy', 'Middle turbinate resection to improve topical irrigation access', 'A Draf III to address any possible frontal contribution to disease'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Revision surgery should correct the mechanism of failure, not repeat procedures. His drainage pathway is already wide and includes the natural ostium, while the periapical lesion explains continuing infection of the maxillary floor. Source control belongs at the dental origin through endodontic treatment or extraction by a dental specialist. Re-dissecting a patent cavity adds scar and risk without treating the cause, unless a separate correctable sinonasal target remains after dental treatment.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Widening an already patent antrostomy leaves the infected tooth in place and will not stop reinfection.', 'Middle turbinate resection adds morbidity and does not address the dental source of infection.', 'His frontal sinuses are not implicated, and maximal frontal surgery does not correct an odontogenic source.'], 'board_pearl': 'Persistent disease in a patent post-ESS cavity: look for a non-anatomic cause such as a dental source.', 'curveball': 'What operative-record and CT findings help distinguish residual uncinate, recirculation, stenosis and nonrhinogenic failure?', 'curveball_answer': 'A retained uncinate on coronal CT with an intact infundibulum suggests incomplete surgery; a separate posterior opening suggests recirculation; circumferential narrowing or neo-osteogenesis at an ostium indicates stenosis. A widely patent, clear cavity with ongoing symptoms points to nonrhinogenic causes such as migraine. Operative notes clarify which sinuses were actually opened.'},
 'v136_rhi_24': {'stem': 'A 38-year-old man has fixed right nasal obstruction since a soccer injury in college. A year of intranasal steroid did not help. Examination shows the caudal septum dislocated off the anterior nasal spine into the right nostril, narrowing the right vestibule; it does not change after oxymetazoline. The inferior turbinates are normal, and lateral wall support does not improve his breathing. Which treatment most directly addresses the cause?', 'choices': ['Inferior turbinate reduction with outfracture on the right', 'Nasal valve dilators with continued intranasal steroid', 'Submucous resection of the posterior bony septum only', 'Septoplasty repositioning the caudal septum, preserving support'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'His obstruction is structural: a caudal septal dislocation that narrows the vestibule and does not respond to decongestion or steroid. Septoplasty is indicated for symptomatic fixed septal obstruction that fails medical therapy. Caudal deformities require repositioning and fixation of the caudal septum to the anterior nasal spine, with scoring, suturing or grafting as needed, while preserving or reconstructing the L-strut to maintain tip and dorsal support.', 'why_wrong': ['His turbinates are normal, and turbinate surgery does not correct a dislocated caudal septum narrowing the vestibule.', 'Dilators do not correct a fixed caudal dislocation, and his symptoms have already failed steroid therapy.', 'The deformity is caudal, and resecting only posterior bony septum leaves the obstruction in place.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Fixed caudal septal deviation needs septoplasty with repositioning and fixation while preserving L-strut support.', 'curveball': 'If lateral wall support had improved his breathing, how would an internal nasal valve problem change the operative plan?', 'curveball_answer': 'A coexisting internal valve problem needs its own correction, because septoplasty alone does not widen the valve angle or stiffen the lateral wall. Spreader grafts or flaps widen a narrow internal valve, while lateral crural strut or alar batten grafts support collapsing lateral walls. These are performed in the same procedure, often through an open approach.'},
 'v144_rh_19': {'stem': 'A 33-year-old woman is undergoing septoplasty for fixed left obstruction from a severe deviation of the quadrangular cartilage and a basal spur. The resident has elevated mucoperichondrial flaps and asks how much cartilage can safely be removed. She has normal tip projection and a straight dorsum, and wants no change in her nasal appearance. Which principle should guide cartilage resection?', 'choices': ['Remove all deviated cartilage, including the caudal strut', 'Preserve a 1-cm dorsal and caudal L-strut of cartilage', 'Preserve only the dorsal strut, since caudal support is minor', 'Resect posterior bone only and leave all cartilage untouched'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The septum is both an airway partition and a structural support for the nasal dorsum and tip. Deviated cartilage and bone can be removed or straightened, but an L-shaped strut of at least about 1 cm dorsally and caudally must be preserved, with a stable attachment at the keystone and the anterior nasal spine. Overresection causes saddle nose deformity, tip ptosis, columellar retraction and valve compromise, which are cosmetic and functional problems.', 'why_wrong': ['Removing the caudal strut destabilizes the tip and risks columellar retraction and tip ptosis.', 'Correct.', 'Caudal support is essential for tip projection and rotation; losing it causes tip ptosis and columellar retraction.', 'Her deviation involves the quadrangular cartilage, so leaving all cartilage untouched would not relieve her obstruction.'], 'board_pearl': 'Septoplasty: preserve a dorsal and caudal L-strut of at least 1 cm to prevent saddle nose and tip ptosis.', 'curveball': 'How does a caudal deviation change fixation and reconstruction compared with a simple midseptal spur?', 'curveball_answer': 'A midseptal spur can usually be resected while leaving the L-strut intact. A caudal deviation involves the strut itself, so it must be repositioned rather than removed: freed from the maxillary crest, scored or reinforced with batten grafts, and sutured to the anterior nasal spine. Severe caudal deformity may need a caudal replacement graft or extracorporeal septoplasty.'},
 'v209_rhi_septum_snr': {'stem': 'During septoplasty in a 45-year-old man with a severe C-shaped caudal deviation after childhood trauma, the attending finds that straightening the septum would require removing most of the remaining caudal and dorsal L-strut. The cartilage is fractured and curved in multiple planes, and scoring has not straightened it. Tip support is currently normal. What is the best decision?', 'choices': ['Resect the caudal strut and accept some postoperative tip settling', 'Abandon septal work and perform inferior turbinate reduction instead', 'Reconstruct with a caudal replacement graft or extracorporeal septoplasty', 'Leave the caudal deviation and resect only the posterior cartilage'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'The septum is both airway partition and structural support. When a caudal deformity cannot be straightened without sacrificing the L-strut, the solution is reconstruction rather than excision: a caudal septal replacement graft, or extracorporeal septoplasty in which the septum is removed, straightened or rebuilt on the bench, and reimplanted with fixation to the dorsum and anterior nasal spine. Removing the strut causes saddle deformity, tip ptosis and valve collapse even if the airway looks straighter on the table.', 'why_wrong': ['Removing the strut risks saddling, tip ptosis and valve collapse, harming both function and appearance.', 'Turbinate reduction does not correct a severe caudal deviation, so his obstruction would persist.', 'Correct.', 'A caudal deviation is the main cause of obstruction, and posterior work alone leaves it untreated.'], 'board_pearl': 'When straightening requires losing the L-strut, reconstruct with a caudal graft or extracorporeal septoplasty.', 'curveball': 'When does a caudal septal deformity favor extracorporeal reconstruction or fixation to the anterior nasal spine?', 'curveball_answer': 'Extracorporeal or caudal replacement techniques are favored for severe, multiplanar caudal and dorsal deformities that resist scoring, suturing and batten grafts, often after trauma. Fixation to the anterior nasal spine through a drill hole or periosteal suture is needed whenever the caudal septum is dislocated off the spine or has been detached during repositioning, to prevent recurrence.'},
 'v136_rhi_25': {'stem': 'A 64-year-old man has 3 months of right nasal obstruction, recurrent right epistaxis and new numbness of the right cheek and upper lip. Endoscopy shows a friable mass filling the right nasal cavity. CT shows an enhancing right maxillary and ethmoid mass eroding the posterior maxillary wall and lamina papyracea. Vision and eye movements are normal. What is the most appropriate next step?', 'choices': ['Endoscopic debulking to relieve obstruction, then send the tissue', 'Contrast MRI to map extent, then targeted biopsy and staging', 'Medial maxillectomy as a combined diagnostic and therapeutic step', 'Six weeks of antibiotics and nasal steroid, then repeat the CT'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Unilateral obstruction, epistaxis and V2 numbness with a destructive enhancing mass suggest sinonasal malignancy, which is histologically diverse. CT shows bone erosion, while MRI distinguishes tumor from retained secretions and maps orbital, perineural, dural and intracranial spread, ideally before biopsy alters the images. Tissue diagnosis then comes from targeted endoscopic biopsy after vascular lesions are excluded, followed by neck and distant staging. Histology and extent then guide multidisciplinary planning.', 'why_wrong': ['Debulking before imaging and histology can cause bleeding, spread tumor and compromise later margin-directed resection.', 'Correct.', 'Definitive resection before pathology and full staging may be the wrong operation for this histology and extent.', 'Cheek numbness and bone erosion are red flags for malignancy; delaying for antibiotic therapy loses valuable time.'], 'board_pearl': 'Unilateral sinonasal mass with numbness or bone erosion: image with MRI and CT, then biopsy and stage.', 'curveball': 'His MRI shows tumor at the orbital apex abutting the cavernous sinus. How does that affect resectability and treatment planning?', 'curveball_answer': "Orbital apex involvement usually requires orbital exenteration for clear margins, and cavernous sinus invasion generally makes en bloc resection with negative margins unrealistic. Such disease is often treated nonsurgically with definitive chemoradiation, sometimes after induction chemotherapy, decided by a multidisciplinary tumor board according to histology and the patient's goals."},
 'v144_rh_20': {'stem': 'A 58-year-old woman has biopsy-proven squamous cell carcinoma of the left ethmoid sinus. She has left epistaxis, obstruction and numbness of the left forehead. CT shows erosion of the fovea ethmoidalis and lamina papyracea. The neck is clinically negative. The tumor board plans to discuss treatment next week. Which evaluation should precede definitive treatment planning?', 'choices': ['No further imaging, as CT has already shown the bony anatomy', 'Contrast MRI for dural, orbital and perineural extent, and PET-CT', 'Repeat biopsy to confirm the histologic subtype before imaging', 'Induction chemotherapy first, followed by imaging to plan surgery'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'CT defines bone erosion, but MRI is essential to distinguish tumor from secretions and to define dural, brain, periorbital and perineural spread, here suggested by forehead numbness along V1. PET-CT or chest imaging stages distant disease, and neck imaging assesses nodes. These findings determine resectability, the need for craniofacial resection or orbital preservation, radiation fields and systemic therapy. Staging-quality imaging and tissue diagnosis both come before definitive multidisciplinary planning.', 'why_wrong': ['CT alone cannot assess dural, orbital soft tissue or perineural involvement, which change surgery and radiation fields.', 'Correct.', 'Histology is already established, and repeating biopsy delays the staging that will actually change the plan.', 'Starting treatment before full staging prevents an accurate baseline and may lead to an inappropriate treatment plan.'], 'board_pearl': 'Sinonasal cancer staging needs both CT and MRI plus nodal and distant imaging before multidisciplinary planning.', 'curveball': 'MRI shows perineural spread along V1 toward the cavernous sinus. How does that change resectability and radiation planning?', 'curveball_answer': 'Perineural spread worsens prognosis and must be followed to its proximal extent. If it reaches the cavernous sinus, clear surgical margins are rarely achievable. Radiation fields must cover the nerve pathway back to the skull base, foramina and Gasserian ganglion as indicated. Surgery is chosen only if the tumor and involved nerve segment can be removed with negative margins.'},
 'v209_rhi_malignancy_snr': {'stem': 'A 61-year-old man has sinonasal squamous cell carcinoma involving the ethmoid and sphenoid sinuses with extension to the right orbital apex and cavernous sinus. He has right vision loss, ophthalmoplegia and V1 and V2 numbness. The anterior tumor is endoscopically reachable, but clear margins would require cavernous sinus and carotid sacrifice. There are no distant metastases. What is the best treatment recommendation?', 'choices': ['Multidisciplinary definitive chemoradiation, as clear margins are unrealistic', 'Craniofacial resection with exenteration and cavernous sinus dissection', 'Endoscopic debulking of the reachable tumor followed by observation alone', 'Palliative care alone, as no tumor-directed therapy offers any benefit'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Operability is not the same as resectability. Cavernous sinus and carotid involvement make a margin-negative resection unrealistic and its morbidity extreme, and positive margins confer little survival benefit over nonsurgical therapy. Definitive chemoradiation, sometimes with induction chemotherapy, planned by a multidisciplinary tumor board, offers meaningful disease control with less morbidity. The deciding clue is that the reachable anterior tumor does not define resectability; the most posterior extent does.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Radical surgery that cannot clear the cavernous sinus adds major morbidity without a curative margin.', 'Debulking without adjuvant treatment leaves gross disease and offers no oncologic benefit.', 'He has no distant disease, and definitive chemoradiation can still control the tumor; palliative care can be integrated alongside it.'], 'board_pearl': 'Cavernous sinus or carotid involvement usually makes sinonasal cancer unresectable; favor definitive chemoradiation.', 'curveball': 'If his biopsy instead showed sinonasal undifferentiated carcinoma, how might treatment sequencing change?', 'curveball_answer': 'SNUC is aggressive and chemosensitive, so induction chemotherapy is often used first, with response guiding the next step. Patients with a good response commonly proceed to definitive chemoradiation, while poor responders may be considered for surgery if resectable. This sequencing is supported by institutional series rather than randomized trials and is individualized by tumor board.'},
 'v136_rhi_26': {'stem': 'A 52-year-old woman with chronic sphenoid sinusitis is scheduled for endoscopic sphenoidotomy. CT shows extensive sellar-type pneumatization with lateral recesses, a dehiscent bony covering over the right carotid prominence and a protruding optic canal along the lateral wall. The resident plans to enlarge the opening widely in all directions. Which structures make superior and lateral enlargement most dangerous?', 'choices': ['Vidian nerve and maxillary division of CN V', 'Optic nerve and internal carotid artery', 'Posterior septal artery and sphenopalatine artery', 'Pituitary gland and sellar floor dura'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The lateral wall of the sphenoid contains the optic canal superiorly and the carotid prominence inferiorly, separated by the opticocarotid recess. In well-pneumatized sinuses these structures can protrude into the lumen with thin or dehiscent bone, so forceful or blind lateral and superior dissection risks blindness or catastrophic hemorrhage. Preoperative CT review of pneumatization, dehiscence and septal insertions is mandatory, and safe enlargement proceeds medially and inferiorly.', 'why_wrong': ['The vidian and maxillary nerves lie in the floor and lateral recess; injury causes dry eye or numbness, not catastrophe.', 'Correct.', 'These arteries are at risk with inferior enlargement, which causes bleeding that can usually be controlled with cautery.', 'The sella lies posterior and superior in the midline; it is not a lateral wall danger during sphenoidotomy.'], 'board_pearl': 'Lateral sphenoid wall: optic nerve above, carotid below, opticocarotid recess between; enlarge medially and inferiorly.', 'curveball': 'In recovery she reports new blurred vision in the right eye. What visual symptom or finding after sphenoid surgery requires immediate evaluation?', 'curveball_answer': 'Any decrease in visual acuity, color desaturation, a visual field defect or a relative afferent pupillary defect after sphenoid surgery suggests optic nerve injury or compression, or an orbital hematoma. It requires immediate bedside examination, removal of packing, urgent CT and ophthalmology review, and treatment such as decompression for hematoma or high-dose steroids in selected cases.'},
 'v144_rh_21': {'stem': 'A 40-year-old man with chronic rhinosinusitis is scheduled for bilateral posterior ethmoidectomy and sphenoidotomy. Reviewing the coronal and axial CT before surgery, the attending points out a large posterior ethmoid cell on the left that pneumatizes superior and lateral to the sphenoid sinus. The resident had planned to open the sphenoid through the posterior ethmoid. Which CT finding most changes the operative approach?', 'choices': ['A concha bullosa of the left middle turbinate', 'An Onodi cell with the optic nerve in its wall', 'A Haller cell narrowing the left infundibulum', 'An agger nasi cell anterior to the frontal recess'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'A sphenoethmoidal (Onodi) cell is a posterior ethmoid cell that pneumatizes superior and lateral to the sphenoid, so the optic nerve and sometimes the carotid lie in its lateral wall. A surgeon entering what appears to be the sphenoid through the posterior ethmoid may actually be in the Onodi cell, with the optic nerve exposed. Recognizing it on CT changes the plan: confirm the true sphenoid low and medially, often through the natural ostium, before working laterally.', 'why_wrong': ['A concha bullosa can narrow the middle meatus but does not put the optic nerve at risk during sphenoidotomy.', 'Correct.', 'Haller cells affect the maxillary infundibulum near the orbital floor and are not relevant to sphenoid entry.', 'Agger nasi cells shape the frontal recess and do not change the safety of posterior ethmoid or sphenoid dissection.'], 'board_pearl': 'Onodi cell: posterior ethmoid cell above and lateral to the sphenoid, with the optic nerve in its wall.', 'curveball': 'To avoid entering the Onodi cell by mistake, where is the natural sphenoid ostium relative to the superior turbinate?', 'curveball_answer': 'The natural sphenoid ostium lies in the sphenoethmoidal recess, medial to the superior turbinate and about 1 to 1.5 cm above the choana. Identifying it transnasally, or resecting the inferior superior turbinate to expose it, confirms the true sphenoid low and medially, away from the optic nerve in the Onodi cell.'},
 'v209_rhi_sphenoid_snr': {'stem': 'During left sphenoidotomy for chronic sphenoid sinusitis in a 59-year-old woman, a thick intersinus septum limits exposure of the lateral sinus. Preoperative axial CT shows the septum inserting directly onto a prominent left carotid canal with thin bony covering. The pus and polypoid mucosa can be cleared around the septum with angled instruments. What is the best operative decision?', 'choices': ['Twist the septum off the carotid with Blakesley forceps', 'Shave the septum flush to the lateral wall with a microdebrider', 'Leave the carotid-attached septum and clear disease around it', 'Fracture the septum laterally to widen exposure of the sinus'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Intersinus septa often insert onto the carotid canal or optic canal, and twisting or fracturing them transmits force to thin or dehiscent bone over the artery, which can cause catastrophic carotid injury. When disease can be cleared safely around the septum, accepting an asymmetric cavity is the right senior judgment. If removal is truly needed, the septum is drilled down carefully under direct vision with a diamond bur, never torqued off.', 'why_wrong': ['Twisting transmits torque to the thin carotid canal and is a classic mechanism of carotid injury.', 'Powered shaving next to a thin carotid canal risks rapid arterial injury without benefit to disease clearance.', 'Correct.', 'Fracturing the septum laterally drives force into the carotid canal and can tear the artery.'], 'board_pearl': 'Never twist a sphenoid septum that inserts on the carotid; work around it or drill carefully.', 'curveball': 'What immediate steps are required if brisk arterial bleeding suggests internal carotid injury during sphenoid surgery?', 'curveball_answer': 'Call for help and alert anesthesia to maintain normotension and blood products. Use two suctions and a two-surgeon technique to place crushed muscle patch or firm packing over the bleeding point. Avoid aggressive cautery or packing that occludes the vessel blindly. Transfer urgently to angiography for endovascular repair or occlusion, then obtain follow-up angiography for pseudoaneurysm.'},
 'v136_rhi_27': {'stem': 'A 44-year-old woman has 8 months of nasal crusting, recurrent epistaxis and a whistling sound when breathing. Over the past 3 months her nasal bridge has collapsed. Endoscopy shows a 2-cm septal perforation with friable, crusted edges and granular mucosa. She denies prior nasal surgery and declines to discuss drug use. She asks for reconstruction of her nose. What is the best next step?', 'choices': ['Etiologic workup with ANCA, biopsy and toxicology before any repair', 'Rib cartilage dorsal reconstruction now to restore nasal support', 'Perforation repair with bilateral mucosal advancement flaps first', 'Saline and mupirocin for 3 months, then reconstruction if healed'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Progressive destructive septal disease with crusting, bleeding and saddling suggests an active systemic or toxic process: granulomatosis with polyangiitis, cocaine-induced midline destructive lesion (often with levamisole-associated ANCA), relapsing polychondritis, sarcoidosis, infection such as tuberculosis or syphilis, or lymphoma. Workup includes ANCA serology, urine toxicology, inflammatory markers, urinalysis, infectious testing and biopsy of the perforation edge. Reconstruction during active disease is likely to fail, so diagnosis and disease control come first.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Grafting into actively destroyed, inflamed tissue risks graft resorption, infection and recurrent collapse.', 'Perforation repair fails in active disease and delays the diagnosis of a potentially life-threatening systemic process.', 'Local care alone does not establish the cause; a progressing destructive lesion requires diagnosis, including exclusion of lymphoma.'], 'board_pearl': 'Destructive septal disease with saddling: diagnose GPA, cocaine injury, infection or lymphoma before reconstructing.', 'curveball': 'Her biopsy is nonspecific, but her ANCA returns positive. Which laboratory and systemic findings would support GPA?', 'curveball_answer': 'PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) is most specific for GPA; MPO-ANCA can occur, and levamisole-tainted cocaine can produce atypical ANCA patterns. Supporting findings include hematuria with red cell casts or rising creatinine, pulmonary nodules or cavities on chest CT, otitis media with effusion, subglottic stenosis, and elevated ESR or CRP. Biopsy showing necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis confirms it.'},
 'v144_rh_22': {'stem': 'A 56-year-old man has 3 months of bloody nasal crusting, nasal pain and a cough with occasional hemoptysis. Endoscopy shows a septal perforation and friable granular mucosa. Chest CT shows multiple bilateral cavitating nodules, and urinalysis shows red cell casts with a creatinine of 2.1 mg/dL. He has never used cocaine, and he has no lymphadenopathy. What is the most likely diagnosis?', 'choices': ['Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA)', 'Sarcoidosis with sinonasal involvement', 'Cocaine-induced midline destructive lesion', 'Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Granulomatosis with polyangiitis classically involves the upper airway, lungs and kidneys. Sinonasal crusting and septal perforation, cavitating pulmonary nodules and glomerulonephritis with red cell casts form the diagnostic triad. Recognizing the systemic pattern matters because renal and pulmonary disease are life-threatening and require urgent coordinated care with rheumatology and nephrology, including PR3-ANCA testing and induction therapy with glucocorticoids plus rituximab or cyclophosphamide. Treating the nose in isolation misses the disease.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Sarcoid causes hilar adenopathy and noncaseating granulomas, but rarely cavitating nodules with glomerulonephritis and red cell casts.', 'Cocaine injury can mimic GPA in the nose, but he denies use, and it does not cause glomerulonephritis or cavitating nodules.', 'NK/T-cell lymphoma is destructive in the midline but does not cause the pulmonary-renal syndrome seen here.'], 'board_pearl': 'Nose plus lung plus kidney (crusting, cavitating nodules, red cell casts): think GPA and check PR3-ANCA.', 'curveball': 'Which biopsy site is often most useful when sinonasal tissue is nonspecific but systemic GPA is suspected?', 'curveball_answer': 'Nasal biopsies often show only nonspecific inflammation and necrosis, with the full triad of vasculitis, granulomas and necrosis in a minority. Biopsy of an involved organ with higher yield, such as the kidney in active glomerulonephritis or a lung nodule, is often more diagnostic. When nasal biopsy is done, take generous deep tissue from the perforation edge rather than crust.'},
 'v210_rhi_systemic_snr': {'stem': 'A 39-year-old woman with destructive septal disease and a progressive saddle nose wants rhinoplasty before her wedding in 4 months. Her ESR and CRP are rising, PR3-ANCA is positive, chest CT shows enlarging pulmonary nodules, and rheumatology plans to start induction therapy for granulomatosis with polyangiitis this week. She breathes adequately through her nose. What is the best decision?', 'choices': ['Proceed with costal cartilage reconstruction now before further collapse', 'Inject dorsal filler now as definitive correction of the saddle deformity', 'Debride the septal remnants to stable margins and graft at the same time', 'Defer reconstruction until remission is sustained under rheumatology care'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Reconstruction during active vasculitis has a high risk of wound breakdown, graft resorption or loss, recurrent collapse and continued tissue destruction. Her rising markers and enlarging nodules show active systemic disease, which is the priority and must be treated with induction therapy. Elective structural rebuilding should wait until the disease has been quiescent on treatment, coordinated with rheumatology. Urgent airway or infectious problems, if present, would be addressed separately.', 'why_wrong': ['Grafting during active GPA risks resorption, infection and recollapse, wasting donor cartilage.', 'Filler is at most temporary camouflage, and injecting into actively inflamed tissue carries infection and necrosis risk.', 'Debridement and grafting in active disease adds trauma to inflamed tissue and is likely to fail.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Reconstruct saddle nose in GPA only after sustained remission; active disease defeats grafts.', 'curveball': 'What duration or evidence of disease quiescence would you want before major framework reconstruction?', 'curveball_answer': 'There is no fixed rule, but most surgeons want clinical and serologic remission for at least 6 to 12 months: no crusting or bleeding on endoscopy, normal inflammatory markers, stable or falling ANCA and stable organ disease, with rheumatology agreement. Autologous costal cartilage is preferred, and patients are counseled that relapse can still compromise the result.'},
 'v422-allergy-anaphylaxis-01': {'stem': 'A 41-year-old man weighing 70 kg receives his weekly build-up dose of subcutaneous immunotherapy for dust mite and cat allergy. Fourteen minutes later he has generalized hives, lip swelling, wheezing and dizziness. His blood pressure is 80/46 mm Hg and heart rate 124. He has no IV access yet. Which epinephrine dose and route are correct?', 'choices': ['0.5 mg of 0.1 mg/mL epinephrine given by slow IV push', '0.3 mg of 1 mg/mL epinephrine given subcutaneously in the deltoid', '0.5 mg of 1 mg/mL epinephrine IM in the anterolateral thigh', '0.7 mg of 1 mg/mL epinephrine IM in the anterolateral thigh'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'This is anaphylaxis with hypotension after SCIT. The 2023 anaphylaxis practice parameter supports intramuscular epinephrine at 0.01 mg/kg of the 1 mg/mL (1:1000) concentration, up to a maximum single dose of 0.5 mg in adults and 0.3 mg in children. For 70 kg the calculated 0.7 mg is capped at 0.5 mg. The anterolateral thigh achieves faster and higher peak levels than subcutaneous or deltoid injection. Doses can be repeated every 5 to 15 minutes.', 'why_wrong': ['IV bolus epinephrine outside cardiac arrest risks arrhythmia and severe hypertension; IM is first-line, with infusion for refractory cases.', 'Subcutaneous injection has slower, less reliable absorption, and 0.3 mg is below the indicated adult dose.', 'Correct.', 'The weight-based calculation must be capped at 0.5 mg for adults; 0.7 mg exceeds the maximum single dose.'], 'board_pearl': 'Anaphylaxis: epinephrine 0.01 mg/kg of 1 mg/mL IM in the lateral thigh, maximum 0.5 mg adult and 0.3 mg child.', 'curveball': 'Five minutes after the first dose he is still hypotensive and wheezing. What do you do next, and when?', 'curveball_answer': 'Give a second IM dose now (repeat every 5 to 15 minutes as needed), keep him supine with legs elevated, give high-flow oxygen, and start large-volume IV crystalloid. Call emergency services. If he remains hypotensive or bronchospastic after two to three IM doses, he needs an IV epinephrine infusion in a monitored setting. Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts only.'},
 'v422-allergy-anaphylaxis-02': {'stem': 'A 19-year-old man who is afraid of needles has skin-prick allergy testing on his forearm. Within a minute he becomes pale, sweaty and nauseated and briefly loses consciousness. His heart rate is 46 and blood pressure 84/52 mm Hg. There is no urticaria, flushing, angioedema, stridor or wheeze. Placed supine with his legs elevated, he returns to baseline within 2 minutes. What is the most likely diagnosis?', 'choices': ['Anaphylaxis with early cardiovascular collapse', 'Biphasic anaphylactic reaction to allergen exposure', 'Hereditary angioedema attack provoked by testing', 'Vasovagal (neurocardiogenic) syncope'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Pallor, diaphoresis, nausea and bradycardia with hypotension that resolve promptly on lying down, without skin, mucosal or respiratory findings, are the classic pattern of a vasovagal reaction triggered by needle anxiety. Anaphylaxis typically causes tachycardia, flushing or urticaria, and bronchospasm, and it does not resolve within minutes with positioning alone. Bradycardia is the deciding clue, although anaphylaxis can occasionally present with bradycardia, so monitoring continues.', 'why_wrong': ['Anaphylaxis usually causes tachycardia with skin or respiratory findings and does not resolve within 2 minutes of positioning.', 'A biphasic reaction is a recurrence hours after an initial anaphylactic episode, which he never had.', 'Hereditary angioedema causes nonpruritic swelling over hours without urticaria or syncope, and it is not triggered within a minute by skin testing.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Bradycardia, pallor and sweating that resolve supine without urticaria or wheeze: vasovagal, not anaphylaxis.', 'curveball': 'Why is misdiagnosing vasovagal syncope as anaphylaxis, or the reverse, clinically dangerous in both directions?', 'curveball_answer': 'Giving epinephrine for a simple faint exposes the patient to tachyarrhythmia, hypertension and anxiety without benefit, and mislabels him as having anaphylaxis, restricting future testing or immunotherapy. Mistaking anaphylaxis for a faint delays epinephrine, the only treatment that reduces death, while airway edema and shock progress. When in doubt with any anaphylaxis features, give epinephrine.'},
 'v459_rhino_anaph_fnd': {'stem': 'A 9-year-old boy weighing 30 kg receives his maintenance subcutaneous immunotherapy injection for grass pollen. Eight minutes later he develops diffuse hives, repetitive cough with wheezing and vomiting. His blood pressure is 82/50 mm Hg. His mother asks the nurse to give an antihistamine first. Which treatment should be given first?', 'choices': ['IM epinephrine 0.3 mg in the anterolateral thigh', 'Oral cetirizine with observation for 1 hour', 'IV methylprednisolone as the initial agent', 'Nebulized albuterol with supplemental oxygen'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Skin involvement plus respiratory, gastrointestinal and cardiovascular symptoms after an injection is anaphylaxis. First-line treatment is immediate intramuscular epinephrine in the anterolateral thigh, at 0.01 mg/kg of the 1 mg/mL concentration, here 0.3 mg, which is also the pediatric maximum single dose. Delay in epinephrine is the main factor associated with fatal reactions. Antihistamines, corticosteroids and bronchodilators are adjuncts that do not reverse airway edema or shock.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Antihistamines relieve itch and hives only and do not treat airway obstruction or hypotension.', 'Steroids act over hours and have no role as initial therapy; they do not reliably prevent biphasic reactions.', 'Albuterol can help residual bronchospasm, but it does not treat hypotension or upper airway edema.'], 'board_pearl': 'Epinephrine IM first in anaphylaxis; antihistamines, steroids and albuterol are adjuncts only.', 'curveball': 'What is the standard weight-based epinephrine dose, and what are the maximum single doses in children versus adults?', 'curveball_answer': 'The dose is 0.01 mg/kg of 1 mg/mL (1:1000) epinephrine IM in the anterolateral thigh. The maximum single dose is 0.3 mg in children and 0.5 mg in adolescents and adults. Auto-injectors come in 0.1, 0.15 and 0.3 mg doses chosen by weight. Doses may be repeated every 5 to 15 minutes if symptoms persist.'},
 'v459_rhino_anaph_app': {'stem': 'A 46-year-old woman with long-standing anxiety about injections receives her first allergy skin-prick test. Within 30 seconds she slumps in her chair, pale and clammy, with a heart rate of 44 and blood pressure of 86/54 mm Hg. Her skin shows no hives or flushing, her lips and tongue are normal, and her lungs are clear. Laid flat with legs raised, she recovers fully in 90 seconds. What is the most appropriate next step?', 'choices': ['IM epinephrine 0.5 mg now in case this is early anaphylaxis', 'Supine positioning and monitoring without epinephrine', 'Serum tryptase now, with epinephrine only if it is elevated', 'Hospital admission for 24 hours to watch for a biphasic reaction'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Immediate onset with bradycardia, pallor and diaphoresis, no skin, mucosal or respiratory involvement, and prompt recovery with recumbency define a vasovagal reaction. Management is supportive: keep her supine with legs elevated, monitor vital signs and observe until fully recovered, while watching for any emerging anaphylaxis features. Epinephrine is not indicated for a simple vasovagal episode. The office should still have epinephrine and a rehearsed protocol ready.', 'why_wrong': ['Without skin, respiratory or persistent cardiovascular findings, epinephrine adds risk without benefit in a vasovagal episode.', 'Correct.', 'Tryptase can support anaphylaxis retrospectively but should never gate epinephrine when anaphylaxis is suspected; here it is not.', 'Biphasic observation applies to treated anaphylaxis, not to a vasovagal faint with complete recovery.'], 'board_pearl': 'Vasovagal reaction after skin testing: supine, legs up, monitor; no epinephrine unless anaphylaxis features appear.', 'curveball': 'Why is this discrimination especially important in an office that performs skin testing or administers SCIT?', 'curveball_answer': 'Skin testing and SCIT are recognized causes of anaphylaxis, so staff must recognize it instantly and give epinephrine without hesitation. They also need to avoid labeling faints as anaphylaxis, which would stop beneficial immunotherapy. Offices need epinephrine, airway and resuscitation equipment, a rehearsed protocol and observation for at least 30 minutes after injections.'},
 'v459_rhino_anaph_snr': {'stem': 'A 27-year-old woman ate a dessert containing hidden peanuts and developed hives, throat tightness and wheezing. Paramedics gave 0.5 mg of IM epinephrine 12 minutes ago. Her wheeze and blood pressure (88/56 mm Hg) have only partly improved. She has asthma. An IV line is running normal saline. What is the most appropriate next step, and what discharge plan should follow once she stabilizes?', 'choices': ['IV epinephrine 1 mg push now, then discharge when her vital signs normalize', 'IV diphenhydramine and methylprednisolone instead of further epinephrine', 'Observe for 30 minutes, then discharge with an oral antihistamine plan', 'Repeat IM epinephrine now; discharge later with auto-injectors and referral'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Epinephrine can be repeated every 5 to 15 minutes when the response is incomplete, and she still has bronchospasm and hypotension. Failure after two or three IM doses calls for an epinephrine infusion in a monitored setting with fluids and oxygen. Her asthma and need for repeated doses raise biphasic risk, so she warrants extended observation. Every patient treated for anaphylaxis should leave with two epinephrine auto-injectors, an action plan and allergy referral.', 'why_wrong': ['A 1-mg IV bolus is the cardiac arrest dose and can cause fatal arrhythmia or hypertensive crisis in a patient with a pulse.', 'Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts and do not replace epinephrine for ongoing hypotension and bronchospasm.', 'Incomplete response with asthma needs more epinephrine and longer observation, and discharge without auto-injectors is unsafe.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Incomplete response: repeat IM epinephrine every 5 to 15 minutes; discharge with two auto-injectors and allergy referral.', 'curveball': 'Why might she warrant longer observation than a patient whose mild single-organ reaction resolved after one epinephrine dose?', 'curveball_answer': 'Biphasic reactions, recurrence without re-exposure usually within about 8 to 10 hours but reported up to 72 hours, are more likely after severe reactions, reactions needing more than one epinephrine dose, delayed epinephrine and in patients with asthma. The 2023 parameter supports individualized, extended observation, often 6 hours or overnight, for such higher-risk patients.'},
 'v425-skull-base-orbital-apex-01': {'stem': 'A 58-year-old woman is admitted in diabetic ketoacidosis with a glucose of 520 mg/dL. Over 24 hours she develops left facial pain, complete left ophthalmoplegia, visual acuity of counting fingers with a relative afferent pupillary defect, and V1 numbness. Bedside endoscopy shows pale, insensate, dusky left middle turbinate mucosa that does not bleed when touched. What is the most appropriate immediate next step?', 'choices': ['Biopsy for frozen section now, with amphotericin and DKA correction', 'MRI of the orbits and sinuses before any tissue sampling is done', 'IV broad-spectrum antibiotics and repeat endoscopy in 48 hours', 'High-dose IV corticosteroids for presumed inflammatory apex disease'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Orbital apex syndrome, with vision loss, ophthalmoplegia and V1 numbness, in a patient with DKA and insensate dusky mucosa is acute invasive fungal sinusitis, usually mucormycosis, until proven otherwise. Angioinvasion causes thrombosis and necrosis that spread over hours. Immediate bedside biopsy for frozen section and fungal stains confirms the diagnosis, while liposomal amphotericin B, correction of acidosis and glucose, urgent imaging and surgical debridement proceed in parallel.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Imaging helps define extent but must not delay tissue diagnosis and treatment of a disease that progresses over hours.', "Bacterial antibiotics do not treat invasive fungal disease, and a 48-hour wait can cost the eye or the patient's life.", 'Steroids worsen invasive fungal infection and hyperglycemia and are contraindicated until fungal disease is excluded.'], 'board_pearl': 'DKA plus orbital apex signs plus insensate mucosa: biopsy now, start amphotericin, correct DKA, then debride.', 'curveball': 'How does her presentation differ anatomically from a pure cavernous sinus syndrome?', 'curveball_answer': 'Cavernous sinus syndrome affects CN III, IV and VI, V1 and often V2, sometimes with Horner syndrome, but typically spares vision because the optic nerve runs in the optic canal outside the sinus. Orbital apex syndrome adds CN II involvement, causing vision loss and an afferent pupillary defect, because the optic canal and superior orbital fissure converge at the apex.'},
 'v431-orbital-apex-01': {'stem': 'A 63-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes and new orbital apex syndrome had bedside biopsy of black, insensate right septal and turbinate mucosa 2 hours ago. Frozen section shows broad, ribbon-like, pauci-septate hyphae with right-angle branching invading vessel walls. MRI shows nonenhancing right ethmoid and turbinate mucosa with orbital apex involvement and no intracranial spread. What is the most appropriate next step?', 'choices': ['Oral voriconazole with repeat MRI in 1 week to judge response', 'Hyperbaric oxygen as the primary treatment before any surgery', 'Urgent endoscopic debridement plus IV liposomal amphotericin B', 'Immediate orbital exenteration before any sinus debridement'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Broad, pauci-septate, right-angle branching hyphae indicate Mucorales, and nonenhancing mucosa on MRI marks devitalized tissue. Survival depends on emergent surgical debridement of necrotic tissue to bleeding margins, high-dose liposomal amphotericin B, and reversal of hyperglycemia and acidosis. Debridement is often repeated. Exenteration is considered case by case when the orbit is nonviable, but it does not replace sinus debridement. Voriconazole has no activity against Mucorales.', 'why_wrong': ['Voriconazole is ineffective against Mucorales and delaying a week allows lethal progression.', 'Hyperbaric oxygen is at most an adjunct with limited evidence and cannot replace debridement and amphotericin.', 'Correct.', 'Exenteration is individualized for a nonviable orbit and is not a substitute for or prerequisite to sinus debridement.'], 'board_pearl': 'Mucormycosis: emergent debridement, liposomal amphotericin B and reversal of host factors; voriconazole does not cover it.', 'curveball': 'How does orbital apex syndrome differ from superior orbital fissure syndrome and cavernous sinus syndrome anatomically?', 'curveball_answer': 'Superior orbital fissure syndrome affects CN III, IV, VI and V1 with preserved vision because the optic nerve passes separately through the optic canal. Cavernous sinus syndrome affects III, IV, VI, V1 and V2, with or without Horner syndrome, and is usually vision-sparing. Orbital apex syndrome combines fissure findings with optic nerve dysfunction at the apex.'},
 'v459_rhino_apex_fnd': {'stem': 'A 62-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has 3 days of right facial pain. He cannot move his right eye in any direction and has complete ptosis. Visual acuity on the right is 20/200 with a relative afferent pupillary defect, and he has decreased sensation of the right forehead. Cheek sensation is normal. Which syndrome best describes these findings?', 'choices': ['Superior orbital fissure syndrome', 'Cavernous sinus syndrome', 'Isolated oculomotor nerve palsy', 'Orbital apex syndrome'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Orbital apex syndrome combines dysfunction of CN III, IV and VI, causing ophthalmoplegia and ptosis, and V1, causing forehead numbness, with optic nerve involvement, causing decreased acuity and an afferent pupillary defect. This combination localizes to the apex, where the optic canal and superior orbital fissure converge. Vision loss is the deciding clue that separates it from superior orbital fissure and cavernous sinus syndromes, which are typically vision-sparing.', 'why_wrong': ['Superior orbital fissure syndrome causes ophthalmoplegia and V1 loss but spares the optic nerve, which travels in the optic canal.', 'Cavernous sinus syndrome typically spares vision and often involves V2, which is normal here.', 'An isolated CN III palsy does not explain abduction loss, forehead numbness or the afferent pupillary defect.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Ophthalmoplegia plus V1 numbness plus vision loss or RAPD equals orbital apex syndrome.', 'curveball': 'In this diabetic patient, what diagnosis must be assumed and emergently evaluated until proven otherwise?', 'curveball_answer': 'Acute invasive fungal sinusitis, most often mucormycosis in diabetics and aspergillosis in neutropenic patients. He needs immediate nasal endoscopy with biopsy of any pale, dusky or black insensate mucosa for frozen section, glucose and acidosis correction, empiric liposomal amphotericin B, urgent imaging and surgical debridement.'},
 'v459_rhino_apex_app': {'stem': 'A 55-year-old woman has 10 days of severe left retro-orbital pain. She has diplopia in all directions of gaze with partial ptosis, and decreased sensation of the left forehead. Visual acuity is 20/25 bilaterally. The resident must decide whether the lesion is in the cavernous sinus, the superior orbital fissure or the orbital apex. Which additional finding would most specifically localize it to the orbital apex?', 'choices': ['A left relative afferent pupillary defect', 'Numbness of the left cheek and upper lip', 'A dilated, nonreactive left pupil', 'A left Horner syndrome with miosis'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'The orbital apex is where the optic canal and superior orbital fissure meet, so apex lesions add optic nerve dysfunction to the ophthalmoplegia and V1 loss of fissure lesions. A relative afferent pupillary defect is an objective sign of optic neuropathy even when acuity is still nearly normal, and it localizes the lesion to the apex. V2 numbness and Horner syndrome point instead toward the cavernous sinus, and a dilated pupil reflects only CN III involvement.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'V2 runs through the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus, so cheek numbness favors a cavernous sinus lesion over the apex.', 'A dilated pupil reflects parasympathetic CN III fibers, which are affected at any of the three sites.', 'Sympathetic fibers travel on the carotid within the cavernous sinus, so Horner syndrome suggests a cavernous sinus location.'], 'board_pearl': 'Optic nerve signs, such as RAPD, separate orbital apex syndrome from cavernous sinus and fissure syndromes.', 'curveball': 'If a cavernous sinus process progresses posteriorly, why can the examination eventually mimic true orbital apex syndrome?', 'curveball_answer': 'The cavernous sinus lies just behind the apex, and disease spreading forward or upward can reach the optic canal or chiasm, producing vision loss. The exam then combines cavernous sinus findings with optic neuropathy. Localization therefore must be reassessed over time, and progressive disease should be imaged with contrast MRI including the cavernous sinus.'},
 'v459_rhino_apex_snr': {'stem': 'A 49-year-old man in diabetic ketoacidosis has 18 hours of right facial pain, a new right afferent pupillary defect, ophthalmoplegia and V1 numbness. The overnight MRI slot is 6 hours away. The medicine team asks ENT to wait for the MRI before doing anything. Endoscopy equipment is at the bedside. What is the most appropriate decision?', 'choices': ['Wait for MRI to define the disease extent before taking any biopsy', 'Perform bedside endoscopy and biopsy now while DKA is corrected', 'Start broad-spectrum antibiotics and reassess the patient in 24 hours', 'Defer intervention until ophthalmology completes formal visual fields'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'New orbital apex syndrome in DKA is presumed acute invasive fungal sinusitis, and it progresses over hours. Senior judgment is to perform bedside endoscopy now and biopsy any pale, dusky or insensate mucosa for frozen section, while correcting acidosis and starting empiric liposomal amphotericin B. Imaging defines extent and guides debridement but must not delay tissue diagnosis. Waiting 6 hours or more risks loss of vision, orbital spread and intracranial extension.', 'why_wrong': ['Waiting for imaging delays a diagnosis that changes management immediately; MRI can follow the biopsy.', 'Correct.', 'Antibiotics do not treat invasive fungal disease, and a 24-hour delay is often fatal or blinding.', 'Formal fields add nothing to the urgent decision; the afferent pupillary defect already documents optic nerve involvement.'], 'board_pearl': 'Suspected invasive fungal sinusitis: biopsy at the bedside now; never wait for imaging to start the workup.', 'curveball': 'Beyond antifungal therapy and DKA correction, what surgical principle applies to debridement in fulminant invasive fungal sinusitis?', 'curveball_answer': 'Debride all necrotic tissue back to bleeding, viable margins, accepting turbinate, septal, medial maxillary or palatal resection as needed. Expect serial debridements every 1 to 2 days until margins stay clear. Coordinate with ophthalmology and neurosurgery for orbital or intracranial extent, and consider exenteration only for a nonviable, blind orbit after multidisciplinary discussion.'},
 'v426-allergy-oas-birch-apple-01': {'stem': 'A 28-year-old woman in Minnesota has sneezing and itchy eyes every April and May. Skin testing confirms birch pollen sensitization. Within minutes of biting a raw apple or pear, her lips and palate itch and tingle for about 15 minutes, without hives or breathing problems. She eats apple pie and canned pears without any symptoms. What best explains her tolerance of the cooked fruit?', 'choices': ['Cooking denatures the heat-labile PR-10 protein', 'Cooking breaks down heat-stable lipid transfer proteins', 'Peeling the fruit removes the allergen-rich skin', 'Sugar in baked fruit blocks IgE binding in the mouth'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'This is pollen-food syndrome. IgE against the major birch allergen Bet v 1 cross-reacts with structurally similar PR-10 proteins in Rosaceae fruits such as apple, pear, cherry and peach, and in carrot, celery and hazelnut. These PR-10 proteins are heat-labile and digested quickly, so cooking, canning or baking destroys the epitopes, and symptoms stay confined to the mouth. Her spring symptoms and raw-only reactions are the deciding clues.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Lipid transfer proteins are heat-stable and cause systemic reactions that persist in cooked foods, so they do not explain tolerance.', 'Peeling reduces exposure to peel-concentrated lipid transfer proteins, but PR-10 is present in the flesh; she tolerates cooked flesh.', 'Sugar does not block IgE binding; heat denaturation of the cross-reacting protein explains the tolerance.'], 'board_pearl': 'Birch pollen plus raw apple itch, cooked apple tolerated: PR-10 (Bet v 1 homolog) pollen-food syndrome.', 'curveball': 'What features would make you refer her to allergy rather than simply reassuring her?', 'curveball_answer': 'Refer for any symptoms beyond the mouth, such as throat tightness, hives, vomiting, wheeze or hypotension; reactions to cooked or processed foods, which suggest heat-stable allergens like lipid transfer proteins or seed storage proteins; reactions to peanuts or tree nuts; or worsening reactions. Prick-to-prick testing with fresh fruit and component testing can clarify risk and need for an auto-injector.'},
 'v426-allergy-oas-ragweed-melon-01': {'stem': 'A 35-year-old man in Ohio has nasal congestion, sneezing and itchy eyes that begin in mid-August and end with the first frost in October. For several years, fresh cantaloupe, watermelon and banana have made his mouth and throat itch within minutes of eating, without systemic symptoms. Cucumber also bothers him slightly. Which pollen most likely drives both his seasonal symptoms and the food reactions?', 'choices': ['Birch tree pollen', 'Timothy grass pollen', 'Short ragweed pollen', 'Mugwort (Artemisia) weed pollen'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Ragweed is the dominant late-summer and fall weed pollen in North America, peaking from August until the first frost. Ragweed sensitization is classically associated with oral reactions to melons (cantaloupe, honeydew, watermelon), banana, cucumber and zucchini, linked to cross-reacting proteins including profilins. Birch pairs with spring symptoms and Rosaceae fruits, grass with early summer and tomato or melon, and mugwort with celery and spices, particularly in Europe.', 'why_wrong': ['Birch causes spring symptoms and cross-reacts with apple, pear, stone fruits and carrot, not mainly melon and banana.', 'Grass pollen peaks in late spring and early summer, not from August to first frost.', 'Correct.', 'Mugwort is a late-summer weed linked to celery, carrot and spice reactions, especially in Europe, rather than melon and banana.'], 'board_pearl': 'Ragweed (August to first frost) pairs with melons, banana, cucumber and zucchini.', 'curveball': 'Why does profilin cross-reactivity tend to produce a broader, less predictable food list than PR-10 cross-reactivity?', 'curveball_answer': 'Profilins are highly conserved actin-binding proteins present in almost all plant cells, so IgE to one profilin cross-reacts across many unrelated pollens and foods, including melons, citrus, banana, tomato and others. PR-10 proteins are more family-specific, so birch-sensitized patients react mostly to Rosaceae fruits, carrot, celery and hazelnut.'},
 'v428-allergy-oas-latex-fruit-01': {'stem': 'A 42-year-old operating room nurse with a documented allergy to latex gloves, which cause hand hives, develops lip swelling and generalized hives within 20 minutes of eating guacamole. A month later, a banana causes throat tightness and wheezing. She has no seasonal rhinitis and no pollen sensitization on skin testing. How does this pattern differ from classic pollen-food syndrome?', 'choices': ['Birch pollen-food syndrome with mild, oral-only risk', 'Ragweed profilin syndrome with a broad food list', 'Lipid transfer protein allergy limited to fruit peel and skin', 'Latex-fruit syndrome with higher systemic reaction risk'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Latex-fruit syndrome is cross-reactivity between latex proteins, especially hevein, and class I chitinases in fruits such as avocado, banana, kiwi and chestnut. It is driven by latex sensitization, not pollen, and the deciding clues are her occupational latex allergy, negative pollen testing and generalized reactions. Unlike pollen-food syndrome, which is usually confined to the mouth, latex-fruit syndrome carries a meaningful risk of systemic reactions, including anaphylaxis.', 'why_wrong': ['She has no pollen sensitization, and her reactions spread beyond the mouth, which is atypical for birch pollen-food syndrome.', 'Ragweed cross-reactivity requires ragweed sensitization, which her negative pollen testing excludes.', 'Lipid transfer protein allergy is not linked to latex, and peeling would not explain her avocado and banana reactions.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Latex allergy plus banana, avocado, kiwi or chestnut reactions: latex-fruit syndrome, with real systemic risk.', 'curveball': 'Besides avocado and banana, which other foods are classically implicated in latex-fruit syndrome?', 'curveball_answer': 'Kiwi and chestnut complete the classic high-risk group with avocado and banana. Papaya, passion fruit, tomato, potato, bell pepper, mango and fig are reported with moderate or lower frequency. Patients should be counseled about these foods individually, and testing guides which need avoidance rather than eliminating every listed food.'},
 'v459_rhino_oas_fnd': {'stem': 'A 24-year-old woman with sneezing and itchy eyes each April reports that raw apples and cherries make her lips and palate itch and mildly swell within minutes. Symptoms resolve within 20 minutes without treatment and have never involved her throat, skin or breathing. She eats apple sauce without problems. She has no latex exposure. Which diagnosis best fits this history?', 'choices': ['Peanut-type systemic IgE food allergy', 'Pollen-food (oral allergy) syndrome', 'Latex-fruit cross-reactivity syndrome', 'Hereditary angioedema, type I'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Pollen-food syndrome, or oral allergy syndrome, is a localized IgE reaction to raw fruits or vegetables in a patient sensitized to pollen. Symptoms begin within minutes, are usually limited to the lips, mouth and throat, and resolve quickly, because the cross-reacting proteins are labile and degraded by cooking and digestion. Her spring rhinitis, raw-only reactions to Rosaceae fruits and tolerance of apple sauce point to birch-related pollen-food syndrome.', 'why_wrong': ['Primary food allergy to stable proteins causes reactions to cooked forms and often systemic symptoms, neither of which she has.', 'Correct.', 'Latex-fruit syndrome requires latex sensitization and typically involves banana, avocado, kiwi or chestnut.', 'Hereditary angioedema causes nonpruritic swelling lasting days without urticaria or a reproducible raw-food trigger.'], 'board_pearl': 'Itching confined to the mouth within minutes of raw fruit, cooked fruit tolerated, with pollen allergy: pollen-food syndrome.', 'curveball': 'Given her spring symptoms and apple trigger, which pollen is most likely responsible, and why does cooking the apple usually eliminate her symptoms?', 'curveball_answer': 'Birch pollen. IgE to Bet v 1 cross-reacts with the homologous PR-10 protein Mal d 1 in apple and similar proteins in cherry, pear and peach. PR-10 proteins are heat-labile and quickly digested, so cooking, canning or baking denatures them, and only raw fruit triggers symptoms.'},
 'v459_rhino_oas_app': {'stem': 'A 36-year-old nurse with latex glove allergy gets itching of the mouth after eating raw banana. Last week, after eating avocado, she developed throat tightness and hives across her chest and arms that resolved with oral antihistamine at home. She has no seasonal allergies, and pollen skin tests are negative. What is the most appropriate management?', 'choices': ['Reassure her, since mouth-limited symptoms seldom progress', 'Advise cooking banana and avocado so they can be eaten safely', 'Start birch sublingual immunotherapy to reduce her cross-reactivity', 'Epinephrine auto-injector, latex precautions and allergy referral'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Her latex allergy, negative pollen testing and a systemic reaction to avocado indicate latex-fruit syndrome rather than pollen-food syndrome. Latex-fruit syndrome carries a higher risk of systemic reactions, and she has already had throat tightness and generalized hives. She needs an epinephrine auto-injector with an action plan, avoidance of the implicated fruits, strict latex precautions at work and in medical care, and allergy referral for confirmation and further risk assessment.', 'why_wrong': ['Her avocado reaction was already systemic, so she is not a mouth-limited, low-risk patient.', 'Cooking reliably helps only labile pollen-related proteins; it should not be relied on to prevent systemic latex-fruit reactions.', 'She is not pollen-sensitized, and birch immunotherapy does not address latex-fruit cross-reactivity.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Latex-fruit syndrome with any systemic symptom: auto-injector, avoidance, latex precautions and allergy referral.', 'curveball': 'What testing modality is preferred over commercial extracts when evaluating suspected pollen-food or fruit allergy, and why?', 'curveball_answer': 'Prick-to-prick testing, in which the lancet is pricked into the fresh fruit and then the skin, is preferred. The relevant fruit proteins, especially PR-10 and profilins, are labile and degrade during commercial extract preparation, so extracts have poor sensitivity. Component-resolved specific IgE testing can further separate low-risk from high-risk sensitization.'},
 'v459_rhino_oas_snr': {'stem': 'A 31-year-old woman has confirmed birch pollen-food syndrome with itching of the lips after raw apple, pear and carrot. Over 5 years, symptoms have never gone beyond her mouth, and she has no asthma or latex allergy. She asks whether she must avoid cooked and canned forms of these foods and whether she needs an epinephrine auto-injector. What is the most appropriate counseling?', 'choices': ['Avoid all forms of these foods and carry an epinephrine auto-injector daily', 'Cooked forms are usually tolerated, and an auto-injector is not routine', 'Undergo an oral food challenge before eating any of these foods cooked', 'Avoid every food listed in cross-reactivity tables, cooked or raw'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Cooking, canning, baking and often peeling denature the heat-labile PR-10 protein and restore tolerance, so blanket restriction of cooked forms is unnecessary and overly restrictive. With isolated oral symptoms over years and no asthma or systemic reactions, routine auto-injector prescription is generally not required, although it is individualized. Treating her seasonal allergic rhinitis may lower symptoms in peak season, and she should return if reactions extend beyond the mouth.', 'why_wrong': ['Cooked forms are tolerated in birch pollen-food syndrome, and an auto-injector is not routinely needed for mouth-only symptoms.', 'Correct.', 'Oral challenges are not needed to eat cooked forms of PR-10 foods in a patient with a clear history of oral-only symptoms.', 'Avoiding foods she has never reacted to causes unnecessary dietary restriction without reducing risk.'], 'board_pearl': 'Pollen-food syndrome with mouth-only symptoms: cooked foods are usually fine, and auto-injectors are not routine.', 'curveball': 'She later develops throat tightness and hives beyond her mouth after eating raw celery. How should management change?', 'curveball_answer': 'Systemic symptoms change her risk category. Celery is linked to birch and to mugwort-celery-spice syndrome, and celery allergens can be heat-stable and cause anaphylaxis. Prescribe an epinephrine auto-injector with an action plan, advise avoiding celery in all forms until evaluated, and refer to allergy for component testing and risk assessment.'},
 'v431-empty-nose-syndrome-01': {'stem': 'A 47-year-old man had bilateral total inferior turbinate resections 4 years ago. He now describes a sensation that he cannot breathe through his nose and feels like he is suffocating, worse when he tries to sleep, with nasal dryness and crusting. Endoscopy shows a wide nasal cavity with absent inferior turbinates and dry mucosa. Two prior physicians told him nothing is wrong. What is the most appropriate initial approach?', 'choices': ['Reassure him that the open airway excludes a physical cause', 'Schedule inferior meatal augmentation implants as first-line treatment', 'Refer him to psychiatry alone, since the symptoms are psychogenic', 'Validate his symptoms and start humidification, saline and emollients'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Empty nose syndrome should be considered when paradoxical obstruction, a suffocation sensation, dryness or crusting follows turbinate surgery despite a visibly open airway. It is a multifactorial neurosensory and airflow disorder, not a fixed stenosis. Initial management validates the complaint, begins humidification, saline irrigation and emollients, and screens for and coordinates treatment of common comorbid sleep disturbance, anxiety and depression, while setting realistic expectations and excluding other causes.', 'why_wrong': ['An open airway is the expected finding in empty nose syndrome and does not exclude it; dismissal worsens distress.', 'Augmentation is considered only after conservative care fails and after a careful workup, not as initial treatment.', 'Mood disorders are common comorbidities, but the syndrome has a physiologic basis and needs nasal care as well.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Empty nose syndrome: paradoxical obstruction after turbinate loss; validate, humidify and treat comorbidity first.', 'curveball': 'What workup tools support, though do not definitively confirm, the diagnosis of empty nose syndrome?', 'curveball_answer': 'A detailed operative history, endoscopy documenting turbinate loss and mucosal status, the Empty Nose Syndrome 6-Item Questionnaire (ENS6Q) with a score of 11 or more supporting the diagnosis, and the cotton test, in which a cotton plug placed along the inferior meatus temporarily simulates turbinate tissue. Other causes, including sinus disease and valve collapse, should be excluded.'},
 'v431-empty-nose-syndrome-02': {'stem': 'A 29-year-old woman with chronic obstruction from inferior turbinate hypertrophy is being consented for turbinate surgery. She has heard about empty nose syndrome and asks the resident why it happens. The attending also asks the resident which surgical approach best prevents it. What is the proposed mechanism of empty nose syndrome?', 'choices': ['Loss of mucosal airflow sensation with altered nasal aerodynamics', 'Fixed obstruction from scar bands between the septum and turbinates', 'Chronic bacterial infection of the remaining turbinate remnants', 'Turbulent airflow from a septal perforation created at surgery'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Empty nose syndrome is thought to result from loss of turbinate mucosa containing trigeminal cold receptors (such as TRPM8), which sense airflow, together with altered nasal aerodynamics: air flows through an enlarged, low-resistance cavity with less contact, warming and humidification. Patients therefore perceive obstruction despite a wide airway. The best prevention is mucosa-preserving submucosal turbinate reduction rather than aggressive or total resection.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Synechiae cause true fixed obstruction visible on endoscopy, whereas empty nose patients have a wide, open airway.', 'Crusting and secondary infection can occur, but infection is a consequence, not the primary mechanism.', 'A septal perforation can cause whistling and crusting, but it does not explain paradoxical obstruction with a wide airway.'], 'board_pearl': 'Empty nose syndrome reflects lost airflow sensation and altered aerodynamics; prevent it with mucosa-sparing turbinate surgery.', 'curveball': 'If a patient with empty nose syndrome has temporary improvement with a cotton test, does that guarantee surgical success?', 'curveball_answer': 'No. Temporary improvement with the cotton test supports selecting a patient for inferior meatal or lateral wall augmentation, but outcome evidence comes from small series, benefit varies and durability is uncertain, with implants sometimes resorbing or extruding. Consent should cover incomplete relief, possible revision and the importance of continued medical and psychological care.'},
 'v459_rhino_ens_fnd': {'stem': 'A 45-year-old woman returns 2 years after bilateral inferior turbinate reduction with extensive tissue removal. She describes feeling that air does not enter her nose, a sense of suffocation, dryness and crusting. Anterior rhinoscopy shows a wide, patent cavity with small inferior turbinate remnants and an intact septum. She has no foul odor, and the crusts are thin. Which diagnosis best explains her presentation?', 'choices': ['Primary atrophic rhinitis (ozena)', 'Septal perforation', 'Empty nose syndrome', 'Internal nasal valve collapse'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Paradoxical obstruction, a suffocation sensation, dryness and crusting after turbinate surgery despite a widely patent airway is the classic presentation of empty nose syndrome, a secondary atrophic process. It reflects loss of mucosal airflow sensation and altered aerodynamics rather than fixed narrowing. The surgical history, intact septum, absence of foul odor and wide cavity are the deciding clues.', 'why_wrong': ['Primary atrophic rhinitis occurs without prior surgery and causes thick, foul-smelling crusts, often with Klebsiella ozaenae.', 'A perforation is excluded by her intact septum on examination.', 'Correct.', 'Valve collapse is a true narrowing seen on inspiration, whereas her cavity is wide and her symptoms are paradoxical.'], 'board_pearl': 'Wide nasal cavity after turbinate resection with a feeling of obstruction and suffocation: empty nose syndrome.', 'curveball': 'What bedside test, despite its limitations, may support the diagnosis and help anticipate response to augmentation?', 'curveball_answer': 'The cotton test: a moistened cotton plug placed in the inferior meatus or along the lateral wall for about 20 to 30 minutes simulates augmented tissue. Improvement in symptoms or ENS6Q score supports the diagnosis and may predict benefit from augmentation, but it is not definitive and has variable predictive value.'},
 'v459_rhino_ens_app': {'stem': "A 52-year-old man has nasal dryness, crusting and a feeling of suffocation 18 months after inferior turbinate reduction. Endoscopy shows a widely patent airway with reduced turbinate tissue. Since surgery he also reports poor sleep, loud snoring, daytime fatigue and a persistently low mood. He asks for surgery to 'rebuild' his turbinates. Which next step best reflects appropriate evaluation?", 'choices': ['Polysomnography alone, since sleep apnea explains his symptoms', 'ENS6Q, cotton test and screening for sleep and mood disorders', 'Psychiatry referral alone because his mood symptoms dominate', 'Augmentation surgery now, with questionnaires scored afterward'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Evaluation of suspected empty nose syndrome combines operative history and endoscopy with the ENS6Q questionnaire, mucosal assessment and a carefully interpreted cotton test, since no single test is definitive. Sleep-disordered breathing, anxiety and depression are common and both contribute to and result from the syndrome, so they must be screened for and treated, including with polysomnography when snoring and fatigue are present. This comprehensive approach precedes any decision about augmentation.', 'why_wrong': ['Sleep apnea may coexist but does not explain his paradoxical nasal symptoms, so nasal evaluation is still required.', 'Correct.', 'Mood disorders often accompany the syndrome, but treating mood alone ignores its physiologic basis.', 'Operating before evaluation and conservative care risks failure in a patient whose diagnosis is unconfirmed.'], 'board_pearl': 'Evaluate empty nose syndrome with ENS6Q, endoscopy and cotton test, and screen for sleep and mood comorbidity.', 'curveball': 'If cotton testing produces clear symptomatic relief, what caveat should be discussed before recommending augmentation surgery?', 'curveball_answer': 'A positive cotton test only shows short-term benefit from added bulk. Evidence for augmentation with cartilage, acellular dermis, hydroxyapatite or other implants comes from small, uncontrolled series, and results vary; implants may resorb, extrude or become infected, and symptoms may persist or return. He should understand that surgery may help but is not a guaranteed or permanent cure.'},
 'v459_rhino_ens_snr': {'stem': 'A 48-year-old woman has had empty nose syndrome for 3 years after aggressive bilateral turbinate resection. Humidification, saline, emollients and treatment of coexisting insomnia and depression have given only partial relief. Her ENS6Q score is 18. Endoscopy confirms absent inferior turbinates, and cotton testing reproducibly reduces her symptoms. She asks about inferior meatal augmentation. What is the best counseling?', 'choices': ['Promise a durable cure, given her clearly reproducible cotton test response', 'Refuse surgery and continue saline and emollients indefinitely', 'Resect the remaining tissue to equalize airflow on both sides', 'Offer augmentation, noting that benefit and durability are uncertain'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'She is a well-selected candidate: consistent symptoms, a high ENS6Q score, failed comprehensive conservative care with comorbidities addressed, and reproducible cotton-test improvement. Submucosal augmentation of the inferior meatus or lateral wall is a reasonable option. Because supporting evidence comes from small series and durability varies, counseling must set realistic expectations and explain that implants can resorb or extrude and that revision may be needed.', 'why_wrong': ['A positive cotton test predicts short-term benefit only, and no evidence supports promising a durable cure.', 'Refusing all surgery denies a reasonable option to a well-selected patient whose conservative care has failed.', 'Removing more tissue would worsen the airflow sensation loss that underlies her condition.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Augmentation is reasonable for selected empty nose patients, with frank counseling about variable, uncertain durability.', 'curveball': 'What preventive principle should you teach a junior resident performing turbinate surgery to avoid causing empty nose syndrome?', 'curveball_answer': 'Preserve the turbinate mucosa: reduce submucosal soft tissue and bone with microdebrider, radiofrequency or bony resection, with outfracture as needed, rather than resecting the turbinate or stripping its surface. Prevention is the most reliable strategy because established empty nose syndrome responds incompletely and inconsistently to treatment.'},
 'v431-silent-sinus-syndrome-01': {'stem': 'A 41-year-old woman noticed over 8 months that her right eye looks sunken and sits lower than the left. She has no history of facial trauma, and no nasal congestion, drainage or facial pain. Vision and eye movements are normal. CT shows an opacified, small right maxillary sinus with inwardly retracted walls, an uncinate process lying against the orbit and a downward-bowed orbital floor. What is the diagnosis?', 'choices': ['Missed orbital floor blowout fracture', 'Expansile maxillary sinus mucocele', 'Silent sinus syndrome from atelectasis', 'Congenital maxillary sinus hypoplasia'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Silent sinus syndrome results from chronic obstruction of the maxillary ostium, typically by a lateralized uncinate. Gas resorption creates negative pressure, causing maxillary atelectasis with inward retraction of the sinus walls and downward bowing of the orbital floor. The orbital volume increases, producing painless, progressive enophthalmos and hypoglobus. Its acquired, progressive course over months, without trauma, and the retracted walls with volume loss are the deciding clues.', 'why_wrong': ['A blowout fracture would follow trauma and show a discrete fracture with herniated tissue, not uniform wall retraction.', 'Mucoceles expand the sinus and displace the globe outward or upward, the opposite of this volume loss.', 'Correct.', 'Hypoplasia is developmental and stable, without progressive change over months or an opacified, collapsing sinus.'], 'board_pearl': 'Painless progressive enophthalmos and hypoglobus with a small, opacified maxillary sinus: silent sinus syndrome.', 'curveball': 'What is the appropriate treatment sequence for her sinus and orbital findings?', 'curveball_answer': 'Endoscopic uncinectomy and maxillary antrostomy restore ventilation and stop progression, taking care because the uncinate lies against the orbit and the orbital floor is low. Orbital floor reconstruction with an implant may be done at the same time or staged, depending on the degree of enophthalmos, diplopia, patient preference and how much the floor remodels after re-aeration.'},
 'v431-silent-sinus-syndrome-02': {'stem': 'A 50-year-old man is referred by ophthalmology for 1 year of progressive right enophthalmos noted in photographs. He denies any sinus pain, congestion or drainage, and he was initially told he might have a tumor behind the eye. CT shows a small, opacified right maxillary sinus with a depressed orbital floor. Why is this condition called silent, and how does that affect diagnosis?', 'choices': ['The sinus appears normal on CT, so an MRI is required to make the diagnosis', 'Progression is so rapid that patients present before symptoms can develop', 'The findings are bilateral and symmetric, which masks facial asymmetry', 'Sinonasal symptoms are absent, so the orbital sign is the presenting clue'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Silent sinus syndrome is called silent because most patients have no sinonasal symptoms despite a chronically obstructed, atelectatic maxillary sinus. They present instead to ophthalmology or oculoplastics with painless enophthalmos, hypoglobus or facial asymmetry. Clinicians must therefore consider a sinus cause for unexplained acquired enophthalmos and obtain CT, which shows the characteristic small, opacified sinus with retracted walls and a depressed orbital floor.', 'why_wrong': ['CT is diagnostic, showing a contracted, opacified sinus with a depressed orbital floor; MRI is not required.', 'The condition progresses slowly over months, and the absence of sinus symptoms is unrelated to speed.', 'The condition is almost always unilateral, and asymmetry is what brings patients in.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Silent sinus syndrome presents with orbital signs, not sinus symptoms; get CT for unexplained acquired enophthalmos.', 'curveball': 'How is silent sinus syndrome distinguished from a maxillary mucocele or neoplasm on imaging?', 'curveball_answer': 'Silent sinus syndrome shows sinus volume loss: inward retraction of the walls, a lateralized uncinate and a depressed orbital floor, with uniform opacification. A mucocele expands the sinus with smooth outward wall remodeling. A neoplasm shows a soft tissue mass, often enhancing, with bone erosion or destruction, best characterized with MRI.'},
 'v459_rhino_sss_fnd': {'stem': 'A 39-year-old man has 6 months of gradually progressive right enophthalmos and a lower right eye. He denies trauma, sinus pain, congestion or prior surgery. He has no diplopia, proptosis or eyelid retraction, and thyroid function is normal. He has no history of cancer. Exophthalmometry shows 3 mm of right enophthalmos. Which diagnosis should be suspected first?', 'choices': ['Silent sinus syndrome', 'Thyroid eye disease', 'Orbital floor blowout fracture', 'Metastatic scirrhous breast carcinoma'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Painless, progressive unilateral enophthalmos or hypoglobus in an adult without trauma or prominent sinus symptoms should first raise suspicion for silent sinus syndrome. Chronic maxillary outflow obstruction creates negative pressure, maxillary atelectasis and downward bowing of the orbital floor, enlarging the orbit. CT of the sinuses and orbits confirms the diagnosis. Scirrhous metastases are a rare but important cause of enophthalmos to keep in mind when imaging is atypical.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Thyroid eye disease causes proptosis and eyelid retraction, the opposite of enophthalmos, and his thyroid function is normal.', 'A blowout fracture requires trauma, which he denies, and would not progress gradually over months.', 'Scirrhous breast metastases can cause enophthalmos, but they are rare and he is a man with no cancer history.'], 'board_pearl': 'Painless progressive enophthalmos without trauma: think silent sinus syndrome first and obtain CT.', 'curveball': 'What is the underlying sinus pathophysiology producing the enophthalmos and hypoglobus in this patient?', 'curveball_answer': 'Chronic obstruction of the maxillary ostium, usually by a lateralized uncinate against the orbit, traps the sinus. Gas is absorbed by the mucosa, creating persistent negative pressure that pulls the sinus walls inward. The orbital floor bows downward into the sinus, enlarging the orbit and allowing the globe to sink back and down.'},
 'v459_rhino_sss_app': {'stem': "A 44-year-old woman has painless progressive left enophthalmos. CT shows an opacified left maxillary sinus, an uncinate process lateralized against the orbital floor, and an orbital floor bowed downward into the sinus. The radiologist's report lists silent sinus syndrome and mucocele in the differential. Which CT finding would instead favor an expansile mucocele?", 'choices': ['Uniform opacification of the maxillary sinus lumen', 'Outward bowing and thinning of the sinus walls', 'Downward depression of the orbital floor contour', 'Lateralization of the uncinate against the orbit'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The key radiographic discriminator is volume. Silent sinus syndrome causes maxillary atelectasis, with inward retraction of the walls and reduced sinus volume. A mucocele is an expansile, mucus-filled lesion that thins and remodels the bony walls outward, displacing the orbital floor upward and the globe outward, often causing proptosis. Opacification and uncinate lateralization occur in both, and floor depression is a feature of silent sinus syndrome rather than a mucocele.', 'why_wrong': ['Opacification occurs in both silent sinus syndrome and mucocele, so it does not discriminate.', 'Correct.', 'A depressed orbital floor is characteristic of silent sinus syndrome; a mucocele pushes the floor upward.', 'Uncinate lateralization is the obstructing mechanism in silent sinus syndrome and does not suggest expansion.'], 'board_pearl': 'Silent sinus syndrome shrinks the sinus; mucocele expands it and pushes the globe outward.', 'curveball': 'If diplopia and orbital deformity are significant, when should orbital floor reconstruction be performed relative to sinus surgery?', 'curveball_answer': 'Reconstruction can be performed in the same operation as uncinectomy and antrostomy when enophthalmos is marked or diplopia is present, which allows one anesthetic. Alternatively it is staged after re-aeration, because the floor may partially remodel and reduce the need for an implant. The choice is individualized by severity, symptoms and patient preference.'},
 'v459_rhino_sss_snr': {'stem': 'A 52-year-old woman with silent sinus syndrome has just had endoscopic uncinectomy and maxillary antrostomy, which re-aerated the left maxillary sinus. Preoperatively she had 2 mm of left hypoglobus, minimal enophthalmos and no diplopia, and she is not bothered by her appearance. The oculoplastic surgeon asks whether to place an orbital floor implant. What is the best plan for her orbital floor?', 'choices': ['Place an orbital floor implant now, as is required in every case', 'Repeat CT in 1 week and implant if the floor has not yet risen', 'Discharge from orbital follow-up, since only the sinus itself was involved', 'Observe after re-aeration, staging floor repair only if still needed'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'Orbital floor reconstruction may be simultaneous or staged and is individualized by deformity, diplopia and patient concern. With mild hypoglobus, no diplopia and little cosmetic concern, observation after re-aeration is reasonable because the restored sinus pressure can allow some remodeling of the floor and soft tissues. Staged reconstruction is offered if enophthalmos, hypoglobus or diplopia persist or bother her, with ophthalmologic follow-up over the following months.', 'why_wrong': ['Reconstruction is not mandatory; mild cases without diplopia can be observed after re-aeration.', 'Floor remodeling occurs over months, so CT at 1 week is too early to judge and would mislead the decision.', 'Orbital follow-up is needed to document any change and to offer staged repair if the deformity persists.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Silent sinus syndrome with mild deformity and no diplopia: re-aerate, observe, and stage floor repair if needed.', 'curveball': 'Why do some patients show orbital improvement after sinus surgery alone, without floor reconstruction?', 'curveball_answer': 'Re-aerating the atelectatic sinus ends the negative pressure that was pulling the floor downward. Over months, the floor can partially rise back as bone remodels, and orbital soft tissues may reposition. Improvement is typically partial and unpredictable, which is why reconstruction can be staged for patients with persistent deformity or diplopia.'},
 'v431-endoscopic-pituitary-01': {'stem': 'A 46-year-old man has a craniopharyngioma with suprasellar extension and is scheduled for an endoscopic endonasal transtuberculum approach. Opening the arachnoid and third ventricle region is expected. CT angiography shows medially positioned cavernous carotid arteries with a narrow intercarotid distance. He has never had nasal surgery. Which step should be planned before dural entry?', 'choices': ['Wait to see whether a leak occurs before deciding on any septal flap', 'Plan conversion to an open craniotomy if any CSF leak is encountered', 'Rely on a lumbar drain alone as the closure plan for the expected leak', 'Raise a nasoseptal flap and confirm carotid positions with navigation'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'The endonasal corridor is bounded by the carotid arteries and cavernous sinuses laterally, the optic apparatus superiorly and the clivus posteriorly. A transtuberculum approach with arachnoid or ventricular opening predictably creates a high-flow leak, so a vascularized nasoseptal flap should be harvested or preserved, and the sphenoid opening should not sacrifice its pedicle, before dural entry. The narrow intercarotid distance makes carotid localization with navigation and Doppler essential before opening dura.', 'why_wrong': ['Wide sphenoidotomy and posterior septectomy can destroy the pedicle before a leak is seen, so the flap must be planned first.', 'Most high-flow leaks are reliably repaired endonasally with multilayer closure and a vascularized flap.', 'A lumbar drain may be an adjunct but does not replace vascularized multilayer reconstruction of a high-flow defect.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'Expected high-flow leak: secure the nasoseptal flap and map the carotids before dural entry.', 'curveball': 'Which surgeon is responsible for which part of this endonasal operation, and why does it matter?', 'curveball_answer': 'In a two-surgeon team, the rhinologist creates the corridor, harvests and protects the flap, and performs or co-leads reconstruction, while the neurosurgeon resects the lesion; both work through both nostrils with dynamic endoscope handling. Shared planning ensures exposure, carotid safety and closure are designed together, which lowers leak and vascular complications.'},
 'v431-endoscopic-pituitary-02': {'stem': 'A 38-year-old woman had brain MRI for migraine that showed an incidental 6-mm nonenhancing sellar lesion without suprasellar extension or chiasmal contact. She has no visual symptoms, galactorrhea, menstrual changes or cushingoid features. Formal visual fields are normal. She asks whether the lesion should be removed endoscopically. What is the most appropriate initial management?', 'choices': ['Endocrine screening and repeat MRI, without surgery for now', 'Endoscopic transsphenoidal resection to obtain a diagnosis', 'Stereotactic radiosurgery to prevent growth of the lesion', 'Empiric cabergoline therapy to shrink the sellar lesion'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Not every sellar lesion requires surgery. A small incidental microadenoma without compression needs screening for hormone hypersecretion, including prolactin and IGF-1 and screening for cortisol excess, and follow-up MRI, typically at about a year. Surgery is reserved for growth, visual compromise, apoplexy or a functioning tumor that needs it. Disease-specific medical therapy such as a dopamine agonist is used only once a prolactinoma is confirmed.', 'why_wrong': ['Correct.', 'Resection of a small asymptomatic nonfunctioning incidentaloma carries risks without clear benefit.', 'Radiosurgery is for residual or recurrent tumor, not small untreated incidental lesions.', 'Cabergoline treats prolactinomas; it is not given without biochemical evidence of prolactin excess.'], 'board_pearl': 'Small noncompressive pituitary incidentaloma: test hormones and image again; surgery is not first-line.', 'curveball': 'What symptoms or findings might prompt evaluation of a sellar or skull base lesion in the first place?', 'curveball_answer': 'Endocrine excess, such as amenorrhea and galactorrhea, acromegaly or Cushing features; hypopituitarism, such as fatigue, hypogonadism or hypothyroidism; bitemporal visual field loss from chiasmal compression; diplopia from cavernous sinus involvement; headache; sudden severe headache with visual loss suggesting apoplexy; or an incidental finding on imaging.'},
 'v459_rhino_pit_fnd': {'stem': 'A 54-year-old woman has a 2.4-cm nonfunctioning pituitary macroadenoma with bitemporal hemianopia on formal fields. Endocrine testing shows mild secondary hypothyroidism, now replaced. She is scheduled for endoscopic endonasal resection by a combined otolaryngology and neurosurgery team. She asks which surgeon will be doing which part of her operation. What is the best answer?', 'choices': ['The neurosurgeon performs all of the steps, and rhinology consults afterward', 'The rhinologist creates the corridor and closes; neurosurgery resects', 'The rhinologist resects the tumor, and neurosurgery closes the defect', 'The endocrinologist directs the extent of resection during surgery'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Endoscopic endonasal skull base surgery is a team operation. The rhinologist creates the endonasal corridor, protects or harvests the nasoseptal flap, and leads reconstruction, while the neurosurgeon resects the lesion, often with both surgeons working simultaneously through both nostrils. Success includes visual recovery, endocrine outcome, carotid safety, durable CSF leak prevention and preserved sinonasal function, not only tumor removal.', 'why_wrong': ['Rhinology is involved from the start, because corridor creation and reconstruction strongly affect safety and leak rates.', 'Correct.', 'These roles are reversed; the neurosurgeon manages the intradural lesion and the rhinologist leads access and closure.', 'Endocrinology optimizes hormones before and after surgery but does not direct the operation.'], 'board_pearl': 'Endoscopic pituitary surgery is a team operation: rhinology builds and closes the corridor, neurosurgery treats the lesion.', 'curveball': 'Which anatomic structures bound the endonasal corridor to the sella and must be localized before dural entry?', 'curveball_answer': 'The cavernous carotid arteries and cavernous sinuses bound it laterally, the optic nerves, chiasm and optic canals superiorly with the planum sphenoidale and tuberculum, and the clivus posteriorly and inferiorly. The carotids are localized with CT angiography, navigation and micro-Doppler before opening dura, because the intercarotid distance can be narrow.'},
 'v459_rhino_pit_app': {'stem': 'A 30-year-old woman has 8 months of amenorrhea and galactorrhea. Serum prolactin is 210 ng/mL, and MRI shows an 8-mm pituitary lesion without chiasmal contact. Visual fields are normal, and she takes no medications that raise prolactin. She was referred to the skull base clinic to discuss endoscopic removal. Which management is most appropriate first?', 'choices': ['Endoscopic transsphenoidal resection as first-line treatment', 'Observation with repeat prolactin and MRI in 1 year only', 'Cabergoline, with surgery reserved for failure or intolerance', 'Fractionated radiation therapy to the sella as initial therapy'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'A prolactin of 210 ng/mL with an 8-mm lesion confirms a prolactinoma once medication causes are excluded. First-line treatment for most prolactinomas is a dopamine agonist, usually cabergoline, which normalizes prolactin, restores menses and shrinks the tumor in most patients. Endoscopic surgery is reserved for intolerance, resistance, apoplexy or progressive compression despite medical therapy, or in selected patients by preference. Radiation is a last resort.', 'why_wrong': ['Surgery is not first-line for prolactinomas; most respond to dopamine agonists alone.', 'Her symptomatic hypogonadism and galactorrhea need treatment, so observation alone is inappropriate.', 'Correct.', 'Radiation is reserved for aggressive tumors that fail medical therapy and surgery, given its risk of hypopituitarism.'], 'board_pearl': 'Prolactinoma: cabergoline first; endoscopic surgery for resistance, intolerance or compression.', 'curveball': 'If she later develops progressive visual field loss despite cabergoline, how does that change the plan?', 'curveball_answer': 'Progressive visual loss despite appropriate medical therapy indicates resistant or enlarging tumor, or possibly apoplexy or cyst formation, and requires urgent MRI and formal fields. It shifts management toward endoscopic endonasal decompression, planned jointly by endocrinology, neurosurgery and rhinology, with continued medical therapy for residual tumor.'},
 'v459_rhino_pit_snr': {'stem': 'During an extended endoscopic transplanum resection of a tuberculum sellae meningioma in a 60-year-old woman, the arachnoid is widely opened and CSF pours into the field after tumor removal, leaving a 2-cm dural defect. A right nasoseptal flap was harvested at the start of the case and parked in the nasopharynx. Which reconstructive approach is most appropriate?', 'choices': ['Single layer of absorbable packing, allowing the leak to seal itself', 'Free fat graft alone with fibrin sealant, saving the flap for revision', 'Staged reconstruction at a second operation once she has recovered', 'Multilayer graft repair covered by the harvested nasoseptal flap'], 'answer': 3, 'explanation': 'A large defect with wide arachnoid opening is a high-flow leak, the setting where avascular grafts fail most often. Senior planning anticipated this by harvesting the nasoseptal flap at the start. Closure uses multilayer inlay and onlay grafts covered by the vascularized flap placed on denuded bone around the defect, supported by absorbable packing and sometimes a lumbar drain. Durable leak prevention is part of surgical success, not an afterthought.', 'why_wrong': ['A high-flow leak will not seal with simple packing and risks meningitis and tension pneumocephalus.', 'Avascular grafts alone have high failure rates for high-flow defects; the flap is the most effective step now.', 'Leaving a high-flow defect open exposes her to meningitis and pneumocephalus and is never planned.', 'Correct.'], 'board_pearl': 'High-flow intraoperative leak: multilayer closure with a vascularized nasoseptal flap, planned before dural entry.', 'curveball': 'What multidisciplinary and perioperative factors should be optimized before this patient reaches the operating room?', 'curveball_answer': 'Complete pituitary function testing with replacement of deficits, particularly cortisol and thyroid, formal visual fields and acuity, MRI and CT angiography for carotid and anatomy planning, and nasal endoscopy to confirm the septum and pedicle are suitable for a flap. Joint review by neurosurgery, rhinology and endocrinology sets goals and the reconstruction plan.'},
}
